window.BOOK_GIT_CHAPTERS = {
  "part-01-introduction/ch01-why-version-control.md": "# Chapter 1: Why Version Control Matters ⏳\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The \"Final_Final_v2\" Nightmare**\r\n\r\nZainab is writing a science project on her computer. Every evening she saves a new copy with a new name. After a week, her folder is full of files called `final`, `final_v2` and `really_final`. Now she cannot tell which file has her latest diagram. She also deleted a good paragraph three days ago, and no copy has it any more. Programmers had the same problem, so they built a tool to solve it.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n📁 My_Homework/\r\n   ├── Science_Project.docx\r\n   ├── Science_Project_final.docx\r\n   ├── Science_Project_final_v2.docx\r\n   ├── Science_Project_really_final.docx\r\n   └── Science_Project_SUBMIT_THIS_ONE.docx\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what **version control** is and why software teams use it.\r\n- Compare saving copies of files with keeping a **Git repository**.\r\n- Describe a **commit** as a saved snapshot of your whole project.\r\n- Explain how Git protects a project from lost files and bad changes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤔 What Is Version Control?\r\n\r\nImagine a website with **500 files** and a team of **5 developers**. If everyone saves copies like `website_final_zainab.html`, nobody knows which copy is the real one.\r\n\r\nA **Version Control System** (VCS) is a tool that keeps the history of a project. Think of it as a **time machine** for your files. It:\r\n\r\n- records every line you add, change or delete,\r\n- remembers **who** made each change and **why**,\r\n- lets you go back to any saved point in the past.\r\n\r\n**Git** is the most popular version control system in the world. This book teaches you Git.\r\n\r\nA **repository** (or \"repo\") is a project folder that Git is watching. Git keeps the whole history inside it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Hiking Trail Checkpoints\r\n\r\nThink of Git like the checkpoints on a long hiking trail:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nStart ➔ Bridge ➔ Lake ➔ Ridge\r\n        (CP 1)   (CP 2)   │\r\n                          │ Wrong path! 🧭\r\n                          ▼\r\n        Walk back to CP 2 and try again ↺\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn programming:\r\n\r\n- Each **checkpoint** is called a **commit**.\r\n- A commit is a **snapshot**: an exact photo of your whole project at that moment.\r\n- If a new change breaks your website at 11:00 PM, you do not panic. You go back to your 10:00 PM commit.\r\n\r\nA commit never changes after it is made. People say commits are **immutable** (cannot be changed). This is why you can trust your history.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Peeking at a Real Git History\r\n\r\nInside a project tracked by Git, this command lists the commits, one per line:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere is what Git showed for a small school library website:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n48b6332 (HEAD -> main) Fix spacing in the library header\r\ne740de5 Add book list table\r\n550fe09 Add student registration form\r\nfd27258 Add project plan and README\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour own list will have different codes, because every project is different. You will set up Git and make your own commits in Chapters 3 to 6.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- **Commit hash (`48b6332`)**: a unique code for each commit. Git makes it from the exact contents of the project, using a maths recipe called **SHA-1**. If even one comma changes, the hash changes completely. The full hash has 40 characters; `--oneline` shows only the first 7.\r\n- **Commit message (`Add book list table`)**: a short sentence written by the programmer. It explains **what** changed and **why**.\r\n- **HEAD**: a pointer (a label) that shows which commit you are looking at right now.\r\n- **main**: the name of the main line of work, called a **branch**. You will learn about branches in Chapter 10.\r\n- The newest commit is at the **top**. The oldest is at the bottom.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n- **Linux**: Thousands of volunteers around the world build the Linux operating system together. They share their changes through Git, often without ever meeting.\r\n- **How Git was born**: Linus Torvalds, who created Linux, wrote the first version of Git in **2005**. The tool the Linux team had been using stopped being free for them, so he built a new one that was fast and shared.\r\n- **Space and science**: Teams that write software for satellites and Mars rovers keep every change under version control. A mistake millions of kilometres away cannot be fixed with a USB stick, so every change must be tracked and checked.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nLook at this scenario:\r\n\r\n1. You make a commit that includes `books.html`.\r\n2. You delete `books.html` from your computer by mistake.\r\n3. You run `git restore books.html`.\r\n\r\n**Question**: Is `books.html` gone forever, or does it come back?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** It comes back. You committed the file earlier, so Git copies it back from its saved history. You will learn `git restore` properly in Chapter 8.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Mystery Bug**\r\n\r\nA student's school website worked perfectly on Monday. On Wednesday, the navigation bar vanished. The student cannot remember which file they changed on Tuesday.\r\n\r\n**How Git solves this**: The student asks Git to show exactly which lines changed since Monday (you will learn `git diff` and `git log` in Chapter 7). In seconds, they find a missing `</div>` tag in `header.html`, fix it, and the menu is back.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Thinking Git saves by itself, like a cloud drive**\r\n\r\n- **Wrong idea**: \"I pressed `Ctrl + S`, so Git has saved my work.\"\r\n- **What happens**: `Ctrl + S` only saves the file on your disk. Git records nothing until you make a commit. If you break the file later, there is no checkpoint to go back to.\r\n- **Fixed idea**: You decide when a checkpoint is ready, and you make a commit on purpose.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Waiting weeks before making one giant commit**\r\n\r\n- **Wrong**: changing 15 files over three weeks, then one commit called `Updated stuff`.\r\n- **What happens**: if something breaks, the only checkpoint is three weeks old, and the message says nothing.\r\n- **Fixed**: small commits often, each with a clear message, such as `Add contact form` or `Fix header alignment`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nYou do not need Git yet. Open any school folder on your computer (or think of your last project).\r\n\r\n1. Count how many copies of the same work you have (`final`, `v2`, `new` and so on).\r\n2. Write a list of three \"checkpoints\" you could have made instead, each with a short message.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nA good checkpoint message starts with an action word: `Add title page`, `Add plant growth chart`, `Fix spelling in conclusion`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Version control keeps the full history of a project, so nothing good is lost.\r\n- Git is the most popular version control system. Linus Torvalds created it in 2005.\r\n- A **commit** is a snapshot of your whole project, like a checkpoint on a trail.\r\n- Every commit has a unique **hash** and a **message** that explains the change.\r\n- Git does not save by itself: you choose when to commit. Commit small steps often.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. What does **VCS** stand for?\r\n2. Who created Git, and in what year?\r\n3. What is a commit?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. **Version Control System**.\r\n2. **Linus Torvalds**, in **2005**.\r\n3. A commit is a saved snapshot of the whole project at one moment, with a message and a unique hash.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why is saving copies like `project_v1.html` and `project_v2.html` risky for a team?\r\n2. How does a commit hash show that saved work has not been changed in secret?\r\n3. Write three clear commit messages for these tasks:\r\n   - Creating a navigation bar for a neighbourhood library site.\r\n   - Fixing a typo on the admissions page.\r\n   - Making the footer fit on mobile screens.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Nobody knows which copy is the newest. Changes in one copy are missing from the others, and there is no record of who changed what or why.\r\n2. The hash is made from the exact contents. If anyone changed even one character, the hash would be different, so the change would be noticed.\r\n3. For example:\r\n   - `Add navigation bar to library home page`\r\n   - `Fix typo on admissions page`\r\n   - `Make footer fit on mobile screens`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\nPlan the history of a small project: a website for a school charity book drive.\r\n\r\n1. List five commits you would make, from first to last, each with a clear message.\r\n2. Mark the commit you would go back to if the last change broke the page.\r\n3. Answer: if your computer switches off while you are typing, can Git get back the lines you never saved or committed? Why?\r\n4. In two or three sentences, explain how the value of **trust (*Amānah*)** connects to keeping an honest, clear Git history for your team.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Example plan:\r\n   - `Add README with project goals`\r\n   - `Add home page with book drive dates`\r\n   - `Add list of books needed`\r\n   - `Add donation drop-off map`\r\n   - `Add thank-you section for volunteers`\r\n2. If the thank-you section broke the page, go back to `Add donation drop-off map`.\r\n3. No. Git only knows about work you have committed. Lines that were never saved or committed are not in Git's history.\r\n4. Your team trusts that the history is true. Clear, honest messages tell them exactly what you changed and why, so nobody is misled. Keeping that record carefully is looking after something people have trusted you with.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch02-how-git-works.md": "# Chapter 2: How Git Works Under the Hood ⚙️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Packing a Parcel**\r\n\r\nBilal is sending a parcel of books to his cousin in Jammu. His desk is covered with books, letters and wrapping paper. He does not tip the whole messy desk into the parcel. He picks only the two books and the letter, and puts them in the box. Then he seals the box and writes a tracking number on it. Git moves your work through the same steps: desk, box, sealed parcel.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Name Git's three local areas: the **working tree**, the **staging area** and the **repository**.\r\n- Explain why `git add` comes before `git commit`.\r\n- Describe what the hidden `.git` folder holds.\r\n- Trace the journey of a file from your editor to a commit (and later to GitHub).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📦 The Parcel and the Three Areas\r\n\r\nEach part of the parcel story matches a part of Git:\r\n\r\n| Parcel story | Git name | What it is |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Messy desk | **Working tree** | The files you can see and edit in your folder |\r\n| Open box | **Staging area** | The changes you have picked for the next commit |\r\n| Sealed parcel | **Repository** | The saved commits inside the `.git` folder |\r\n| Courier van | **Remote** (e.g. GitHub) | A copy of the repository on another computer |\r\n\r\nThe staging area is also called the **index**. The first three areas are all on your own computer. That is why they are called **local**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Journey of a Change\r\n\r\n![Git Three Trees & GitHub Remote](../../assets/diagrams/git/git-three-trees.svg)\r\n\r\n```text\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ 1. WORKING TREE          │\r\n│    files you edit        │\r\n└────────────┬─────────────┘\r\n             │ git add\r\n             ▼\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ 2. STAGING AREA          │\r\n│    picked for next save  │\r\n└────────────┬─────────────┘\r\n             │ git commit\r\n             ▼\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ 3. LOCAL REPOSITORY      │\r\n│    commits in .git       │\r\n└────────────┬─────────────┘\r\n             │ git push (Ch 14)\r\n             ▼\r\n┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n│ 4. REMOTE (GitHub)       │\r\n│    shared online copy    │\r\n└──────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Watching a File Move\r\n\r\nAyesha has a school website that already has one commit. She adds a welcome banner to `index.html` and also makes a new file, `logo.png`. She asks Git what it sees:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\tlogo.png\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth changes are only in the **working tree**. Now she puts `index.html` in the box (the staging area):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\tlogo.png\r\n```\r\n\r\n`index.html` is now under **Changes to be committed** (green in most terminals). `logo.png` is still **untracked** (red), which means Git is not looking after it yet. Finally, she seals the box:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"Add welcome banner to home page\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main e1b4138] Add welcome banner to home page\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOnly `index.html` went into the commit. Later, `git push` sends commits to GitHub. You will learn that in Chapter 14. You will learn each command step by step in Chapters 4 to 6.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Why Have a Staging Area?\r\n\r\nMany beginners ask: *\"Why must I run `git add` before `git commit`?\"*\r\n\r\nImagine you did two jobs this afternoon:\r\n\r\n1. You built a new menu in `navbar.html`.\r\n2. You fixed a spelling mistake in `about.html`.\r\n\r\nThe staging area lets you save them as **two separate commits**:\r\n\r\n- Stage only `navbar.html`, then commit `Add navigation menu`.\r\n- Stage `about.html`, then commit `Fix spelling in principal's welcome note`.\r\n\r\nEach commit does one job. Your history stays clean and easy for others to read. You also never save something by accident, such as a file with a password in it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**The hidden `.git` folder**\r\n\r\nWhen you turn a folder into a Git repository, Git makes a hidden folder called `.git` inside it.\r\n\r\n- Everything Git knows is inside `.git`: every commit, every branch and the staging area.\r\n- If you copy your project **without** `.git`, you copy the files but lose all the history.\r\n- If `.git` is safe, you can bring back any committed file, even after deleting it from the folder.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nA developer changes two files: `contact.html` and `secrets.env`. They run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add contact.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Update office phone number\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Question**: Is `secrets.env` in the new commit?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** No. Only files in the staging area go into a commit. `secrets.env` was never added, so it stays in the working tree and is not saved by Git.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Red and Green Files**\r\n\r\nA student types `git status` and sees:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\tlogo.png\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Green `index.html`**: it is in the **staging area**, ready for the next commit.\r\n- **Red `logo.png`**: it is only in the **working tree**. Git has not been told to track it yet.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Committing without adding first**\r\n\r\n**Wrong:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"Add banner\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**What happens:** nothing is saved. Git shows:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n(If the only new files are untracked, the last line says `nothing added to commit but untracked files present (use \"git add\" to track)`.)\r\n\r\n**Fixed:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Add banner\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Deleting the `.git` folder**\r\n\r\n- **Wrong idea**: \"`.git` looks like junk. I will delete it to tidy up.\"\r\n- **What happens**: your folder stops being a Git repository. All commits and history are gone.\r\n- **Fixed idea**: leave `.git` alone. It is Git's memory.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nDraw the parcel journey on paper (or in a notes app) for a real task. Choose a file such as `timetable.html`.\r\n\r\n1. Draw four boxes: working tree, staging area, repository, remote.\r\n2. Write the command on each arrow between them.\r\n3. Write where `timetable.html` is after each command.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nThe arrows are `git add`, then `git commit`, then `git push`. After `git add`, the file's change is in the staging area. After `git commit`, it is saved in `.git`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Git has three local areas: the working tree, the staging area and the repository.\r\n- `git add` puts chosen changes in the staging area (the box).\r\n- `git commit` seals the staged changes into a permanent snapshot.\r\n- Everything Git knows lives in the hidden `.git` folder.\r\n- A remote such as GitHub is a shared copy you send commits to with `git push`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Name Git's three local areas in order.\r\n2. Which command moves a changed file from the working tree to the staging area?\r\n3. Which hidden folder holds Git's history?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Working tree → staging area → local repository.\r\n2. `git add`, for example `git add index.html`.\r\n3. The `.git` folder.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Explain the difference between `git add` and `git commit` with your own everyday example.\r\n2. Why is a staging area safer than saving every change straight into the history?\r\n3. You edited `app.js` and `notes.txt`, but only `app.js` is ready. Which commands save only `app.js`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Example: packing a school bag. `git add` is putting chosen books into the bag. `git commit` is zipping the bag and writing the date on a label.\r\n2. You choose exactly what goes in. Half-finished work or private files are not saved by accident.\r\n3. Stage only `app.js`, then commit:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add app.js\r\ngit commit -m \"Add search to library page\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n1. You edit `index.html`, run `git add index.html`, then edit `index.html` **again** before committing. Predict what `git status` shows.\r\n2. Which version of the file would `git commit` save: the first edit, or both edits?\r\n3. Write a short rule (3–4 lines) for your coding club explaining why passwords and private student data must never be added to any Git commit (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. The file appears **twice**: once under \"Changes to be committed\" and once under \"Changes not staged for commit\":\r\n\r\n   ```text\r\n   Changes to be committed:\r\n     (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n   \tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\n   Changes not staged for commit:\r\n     (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n     (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n   \tmodified:   index.html\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n2. Only the first edit, because that is what was staged. Run `git add index.html` again to include the second edit.\r\n3. Example rule: \"Passwords and student details are a trust (*Amānah*). Once they are in a commit, anyone with a copy of the repository can read them. Keep them in files that Git ignores. If a secret is ever committed, tell your teacher and change it.\"\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-introduction/ch03-setting-up-git.md": "# Chapter 3: Setting Up Git & Your Identity 💻\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Signing Your Work**\r\n\r\nWhen an architect draws the plan for a new school, she signs and dates it. Everyone then knows who made the plan and who can answer questions about it. Git does the same thing for code. Every commit is stamped with a name, an email address and the date and time. So before your first commit, you must tell Git who you are.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Check that Git is installed with `git --version`.\r\n- Set your name and email with `git config --global`.\r\n- Set the first branch name of new projects to `main`.\r\n- Read your settings back and fix a wrong one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ Installing Git\r\n\r\nGit is free. Download it from the official site, **git-scm.com**, or ask your teacher to install it in the computer lab.\r\n\r\n- **Windows**: run the installer and keep the default choices. It also installs **Git Bash**, a terminal for Git.\r\n- **macOS**: type `git --version` in Terminal. If Git is missing, the Mac offers to install it.\r\n- **Linux (Ubuntu)**: `sudo apt install git` (ask an adult or teacher before installing software).\r\n\r\nThen open a terminal (PowerShell, Command Prompt, Terminal or Git Bash) and check:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit --version\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\ngit version 2.54.0.windows.1\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour number may be different. Any recent version works with this book.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ Your Git \"Passport\": the `.gitconfig` File\r\n\r\nGit keeps your personal settings in a file called `.gitconfig` in your home folder. Think of it as a passport that Git reads every time you commit:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n┌────────────────────────────────┐\r\n│ ~/.gitconfig                   │\r\n├────────────────────────────────┤\r\n│ [user]                         │\r\n│     name = Zainab Ahmed        │\r\n│     email = zainab@example.org │\r\n│ [init]                         │\r\n│     defaultBranch = main       │\r\n└────────────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\n`~` means \"your home folder\", for example `C:\\Users\\Zainab` on Windows. When you run `git commit`, Git copies your name and email from this file into the commit.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: One-Time Setup\r\n\r\nRun these three commands once on your computer. Use your own name and email.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --global user.name \"Zainab Ahmed\"\r\ngit config --global user.email \"zainab@example.org\"\r\ngit config --global init.defaultBranch main\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese commands print nothing when they work. Check your settings like this:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --list --global\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nuser.name=Zainab Ahmed\r\nuser.email=zainab@example.org\r\ninit.defaultbranch=main\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit shows setting names in small letters, so `defaultBranch` appears as `defaultbranch`. That is normal.\r\n\r\n> **Note:** Plain `git config --list` shows *all* settings, including many that came with Git. It is a longer list, but your three lines are in it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- `git config`: reads or changes Git settings.\r\n- `--global`: the setting applies to **every** Git project for your user on this computer. You only do this once.\r\n- `user.name`: the name shown next to your commits.\r\n- `user.email`: the email stored in your commits. Later, GitHub uses it to link commits to your account.\r\n- `init.defaultBranch main`: new projects start with a branch called `main`. Without it, Git still uses the older name `master` and prints a hint. This book uses `main`.\r\n\r\n**Keep your email private (*Amānah*).** Every commit you share shows its email to anyone who can see the project. Use a school email your teacher gives you, or, once you have a GitHub account, the private \"noreply\" address GitHub offers in its email settings. Never put a parent's phone number or home address anywhere in Git.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n- When people fix bugs in big open-source projects such as Python or Linux, their names stay in the history for many years.\r\n- Being honest about who wrote what reflects the value of **truthfulness (*Ṣidq*)**. Never commit under another person's name.\r\n- On a shared lab computer, check `git config user.name` first. The last student's name might still be set.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nAfter the setup above, you type:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config user.name\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Question**: What does Git print?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** It prints the name you saved, and changes nothing:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nZainab Ahmed\r\n```\r\n\r\nGiving a setting name with no value means \"show me this setting\".\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Anonymous Commit**\r\n\r\nA student tried to commit on a new computer and saw this:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAuthor identity unknown\r\n\r\n*** Please tell me who you are.\r\n\r\nRun\r\n\r\n  git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\"\r\n  git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\r\n\r\nto set your account's default identity.\r\nOmit --global to set the identity only in this repository.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **What happened?** Git will not make a commit without a name and email. Every change must have an owner.\r\n- **The fix**: run the two `git config --global` commands with your own details, then run `git commit` again.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Forgetting quotes around a name with a space**\r\n\r\n**Wrong:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --global user.name Zainab Ahmed\r\n```\r\n\r\n**What happens:** there is no error, but Git saves only the first word:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nZainab\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fixed:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --global user.name \"Zainab Ahmed\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Typing a made-up email** such as `asdf@fake.com` is a second common slip. Your commits will not be linked to your account later. Use a real email you are allowed to use, as explained above.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOn your own computer (or the lab computer your teacher gives you):\r\n\r\n1. Run `git --version`.\r\n2. Set your name, email and `init.defaultBranch main`.\r\n3. Run `git config --list --global` and check all three lines.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nIf a line is wrong, just run the same `git config --global ...` command again with the right value. The new value replaces the old one.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git --version` checks that Git is installed.\r\n- Git needs your name and email before your first commit.\r\n- `--global` saves a setting once for all your projects, in `~/.gitconfig`.\r\n- `init.defaultBranch main` makes new projects start on `main`.\r\n- Put quotes around values with spaces, and keep personal details private.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which flag makes a `git config` setting apply to all your projects?\r\n2. Which command lists your global settings?\r\n3. What branch name does this book use for new projects?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `--global`\r\n2. `git config --list --global` (or `git config --list` for every setting)\r\n3. `main`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why does Git need an email address before you can commit?\r\n2. What is the difference between `--global` settings and settings made inside one project with `--local`?\r\n3. Write the command that changes your saved email to `zainab.ahmed@example.org`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Every commit records who made it, so the team knows whom to ask about a change.\r\n2. `--global` applies to all projects for your user. `--local` applies only to the one repository you are in, and it wins over the global value there. (`--local` is the default when you are inside a repository and give no flag.)\r\n3. Run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --global user.email \"zainab.ahmed@example.org\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\nWrite a one-page setup guide for students in your computer lab.\r\n\r\n1. Explain how to install Git on Windows, macOS and Ubuntu Linux.\r\n2. List the three `git config --global` commands, with placeholders for name and email.\r\n3. Add a \"check it worked\" step.\r\n4. Add a privacy tip about emails.\r\n5. Extra question: if you change your email next month, do your **old** commits change too? Why?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Lab Setup Guide**\r\n\r\n1. Install: Windows, download from git-scm.com and keep the defaults. macOS, type `git --version` and accept the install. Ubuntu, a teacher runs `sudo apt install git`.\r\n2. Configure:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\r\ngit config --global user.email \"your-school-email@example.org\"\r\ngit config --global init.defaultBranch main\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Check: `git config --list --global` shows the three lines.\r\n4. Privacy: use your school email, never a parent's personal details.\r\n5. No. Old commits keep the old email. A commit never changes after it is made; the new email is used only for new commits.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-essential-workflow/ch04-git-init-and-status.md": "# Chapter 4: Initializing Repositories & Checking Status 📁\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Founding a Club Archive**\r\n\r\nYour class starts a new Science & Coding Club. You do not toss meeting notes into random corners of the room. Instead, you buy one sturdy binder, write the club's name on the front, and put it on a special shelf. From that day on, every official club paper goes into that binder. Git works the same way: it does not watch a folder until you say, *\"Git, please look after this folder.\"* The command that says this is **`git init`**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Turn an ordinary folder into a Git repository with `git init`.\r\n- Explain what a **repository** (or \"repo\") is and what the hidden `.git` folder does.\r\n- Read the messages that `git status` prints.\r\n- Tell the difference between **untracked** and **tracked** files.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ Before and After `git init`\r\n\r\nA **repository** is a project folder that Git looks after. Git keeps all the history in one hidden folder called `.git`.\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBEFORE git init:\r\n📁 eco-club/\r\n   ├── index.html\r\n   └── style.css\r\n   (An ordinary folder. No history.)\r\n\r\nAFTER git init:\r\n📁 eco-club/\r\n   ├── 📁 .git/   <-- Git's engine room\r\n   ├── index.html\r\n   └── style.css\r\n   (A Git repository. History is on!)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `.git` folder holds every snapshot, every branch name and every setting for this project. You never edit it by hand. If you delete it, the folder becomes ordinary again and all its history is gone.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Creating Your First Repository\r\n\r\nOpen your terminal in your practice folder and type these commands:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir eco-club\r\ncd eco-club\r\ngit init\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit replies:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nInitialized empty Git repository in C:/Users/zainab/practice/eco-club/.git/\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour path will be different. Now ask Git how things look:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nnothing to commit (create/copy files and use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\n> **Why `main`?** In Chapter 3 you ran `git config --global init.defaultBranch main`. That setting makes every new repository start on a branch called `main`. Without it, Git still uses the older name `master` for the first branch.\r\n\r\nNow create a file and check the status again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\necho \"# Eco Club\" > README.md\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\tREADME.md\r\n\r\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present (use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn most terminals, `README.md` appears in **red**. Red in `git status` means \"Git sees this, but has not saved it yet.\"\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- **`git init`** is short for *initialize* (to set something up for the first time). It creates the hidden `.git` folder and the starting branch.\r\n- **`git status`** is the most useful command in Git. Run it before and after almost every step. It tells you:\r\n  1. Which branch you are on.\r\n  2. Which files are new or changed.\r\n  3. Which files are staged (ready to save) and which are not.\r\n- **Untracked file**: a file in your folder that Git has never saved. If you delete it, Git cannot bring it back.\r\n- **Tracked file**: a file that is part of at least one commit. Git knows its history and can restore it.\r\n\r\nGit always gives a hint in brackets, such as `(use \"git add <file>...\" ...)`. Read these hints. They tell you the next command you probably need.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**How many `.git` folders should a project have?**\r\n\r\n- The rule is simple: **one `.git` folder per project**, in the project's top folder.\r\n- If you run `git init` in a big folder such as `C:\\Users\\YourName`, Git will try to watch every file in your user folder. `git status` becomes slow and fills with hundreds of untracked files.\r\n- Do not create a repository inside another repository. Professional teams have special tools for that (called *submodules*), but beginners do not need them.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou create a new empty folder. You run `git status` **before** running `git init`. What happens?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\nGit prints an error:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nfatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit commands like `status` only work inside a repository. Run `git init` first.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Invisible Repository**\r\n\r\nA student runs `git init`, opens File Explorer and says: *\"I don't see any `.git` folder! Did it fail?\"*\r\n\r\n- **The clue:** names that start with a dot are usually hidden. This protects important folders from being deleted by accident.\r\n- **How to see it:**\r\n  - Windows File Explorer: **View** → **Show** → tick **Hidden items**.\r\n  - In PowerShell: `ls -Force`. In Git Bash: `ls -a`.\r\n\r\nIn Git Bash, `ls -a` shows:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n.\r\n..\r\n.git\r\nREADME.md\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit did not fail. The folder was there all along.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Running `git init` in the wrong folder**\r\n\r\n❌ **Wrong:** you open a terminal, it starts in your user folder, and you type `git init` straight away.\r\n\r\n**What happens:** Git turns your whole user folder into a repository. `git status` lists hundreds of untracked files, such as `Desktop/`, `Documents/` and `Downloads/`.\r\n\r\n✅ **Fixed version:** check where you are first, then move into your project folder.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npwd\r\ncd eco-club\r\ngit init\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf you already ran `git init` in the wrong place, delete only the hidden `.git` folder there. Your files stay safe; only Git's tracking is removed.\r\n\r\n**Running `git init` twice in the same folder** is harmless. Git just says `Reinitialized existing Git repository in .../eco-club/.git/` and keeps your history.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\n1. Make a folder called `library-notes` and move into it.\r\n2. Run `git init`.\r\n3. Run `git status` and read every line.\r\n4. Create a file called `books.txt` with one book title inside.\r\n5. Run `git status` again. Which section is `books.txt` listed under?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nUse `mkdir library-notes`, then `cd library-notes`. To create the file, type `echo \"The Water Cycle\" > books.txt`. The file should appear under **Untracked files**.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git init` turns an ordinary folder into a Git repository.\r\n- All history lives in the hidden `.git` folder. Deleting it removes the history.\r\n- `git status` tells you your branch and which files are new, changed or staged.\r\n- **Untracked** files are files Git has never saved.\r\n- Keep one repository per project, and check your folder before running `git init`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which command turns an ordinary folder into a Git repository?\r\n2. Which command shows the current state of your project's files?\r\n3. What is an \"untracked file\"?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git init`\r\n2. `git status`\r\n3. A file that is in your project folder, but Git has never saved it in a commit. Git cannot restore it if it is lost.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why do developers call `git status` their \"compass\"?\r\n2. Why is the `.git` folder hidden by default?\r\n3. Create a folder named `weather-diary`, turn it into a repository, create `app.js`, and run `git status`. Write down what Git says about `app.js`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. It tells you where you are (the branch) and what state every file is in. It also suggests the next command. You can always find your way with it.\r\n2. To protect it. If someone deleted it by accident, the whole history of the project would be lost.\r\n3. Commands:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir weather-diary\r\ncd weather-diary\r\ngit init\r\necho \"console.log('Hello');\" > app.js\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n`app.js` is listed under **Untracked files**, in red in most terminals.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n**Clean up and start again properly.**\r\n\r\n1. You accidentally ran `git init` on your `Desktop`. Explain how to undo it without losing any desktop files.\r\n2. Then write the list of commands that would:\r\n   - create a new folder called `clean-start`,\r\n   - turn it into a repository,\r\n   - create a `README.md` with a title line,\r\n   - and print the status.\r\n3. Bonus: put those commands into a script file (`start.sh` for Git Bash) so one command sets up a new project.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Delete only the hidden `.git` folder on the Desktop. In Git Bash: `rm -rf ~/Desktop/.git`. The desktop files are not touched; only Git's tracking disappears.\r\n2. and 3. A script `start.sh`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir clean-start\r\ncd clean-start\r\ngit init\r\necho \"# Clean Start\" > README.md\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\nRun it with `bash start.sh`. The last lines show `README.md` under **Untracked files**.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-essential-workflow/ch05-staging-and-gitignore.md": "# Chapter 5: Staging Changes & The Sacred `.gitignore` 🛡️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Suitcase and the Bin**\r\n\r\nYou are packing for a school science trip. Into the suitcase go your notebook, your slides and your toothbrush. The banana peel from breakfast and your scrap paper go in the bin, not the suitcase. Your house keys and your private diary stay at home. In Git, **`git add`** puts chosen files into the suitcase, and **`.gitignore`** is the list of things that must never be packed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Stage one file with `git add <file>` and everything with `git add .`.\r\n- Unstage a file without losing your work, using `git restore --staged`.\r\n- Create a **`.gitignore`** file that keeps secrets and junk out of Git.\r\n- Stop tracking a file that was committed by mistake, using `git rm --cached`.\r\n- Explain why guarding passwords and keys is a trust (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Staging Filter and the `.gitignore` Shield\r\n\r\n**Staging** means choosing which changes will go into the next commit. The **staging area** is the waiting room for those changes (you met it in Chapter 2).\r\n\r\n```text\r\nYour folder           What happens\r\n-----------           ------------\r\nindex.html  -- add -->  STAGING AREA\r\nstyle.css   -- add -->  STAGING AREA\r\n.env        --+\r\ndebug.log   --+-->  .gitignore shield\r\nnode_modules/ +     (Git skips them)\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Staging Files\r\n\r\nWe start in a small school library project. It has a page, a stylesheet, a secret file `.env` and a log file.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\t.env\r\n\tdebug.log\r\n\tindex.html\r\n\tstyle.css\r\n\r\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present (use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\nStage just one file, then look again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git rm --cached <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tnew file:   index.html\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\t.env\r\n\tdebug.log\r\n\tstyle.css\r\n```\r\n\r\n`index.html` moved to **Changes to be committed** (green in most terminals). That is the staging area.\r\n\r\n`git add .` stages **everything** in this folder and its sub-folders. The dot `.` means \"this folder\". Do not run it yet: `.env` holds a password, so we must build the shield first.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛡️ Example: Creating a `.gitignore` File\r\n\r\nCreate a plain text file named exactly `.gitignore` (with the dot, and no `.txt` at the end) in the top folder of the project:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n# Secret settings (passwords and keys)\r\n.env\r\n\r\n# Log files made by programs\r\n*.log\r\n\r\n# Downloaded libraries (can be installed again)\r\nnode_modules/\r\nvendor/\r\n\r\n# Operating system junk\r\n.DS_Store\r\nThumbs.db\r\n```\r\n\r\nLines starting with `#` are comments. Now check the status again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git rm --cached <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tnew file:   index.html\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\t.gitignore\r\n\tstyle.css\r\n```\r\n\r\n`.env` and `debug.log` have disappeared from the list. Git now ignores them. Now `git add .` is safe:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add .\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\n\r\nNo commits yet\r\n\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git rm --cached <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tnew file:   .gitignore\r\n\tnew file:   index.html\r\n\tnew file:   style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf you try to add an ignored file by name, Git warns you:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add debug.log\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nThe following paths are ignored by one of your .gitignore files:\r\ndebug.log\r\nhint: Use -f if you really want to add them.\r\nhint: Disable this message with \"git config set advice.addIgnoredFile false\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ↩️ Example: Unstaging a File\r\n\r\nSay the project already has one commit. You edit `index.html`, stage it, and then change your mind:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n```\r\n\r\nTake it back out of the staging area:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore --staged index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour edit is still in the file. Only the \"packed\" mark was removed.\r\n\r\n> **Before the first commit**, `git restore --staged` does not work yet. Git says `fatal: could not resolve 'HEAD'`, because there is no commit to compare with. Use the command Git suggests instead: `git rm --cached index.html`. It also keeps your file.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- `git add <file>`: copies the file's current content into the staging area.\r\n- `git add .`: stages every new or changed file in this folder and below, except ignored ones.\r\n- `git restore --staged <file>`: unstages the file. The text you wrote is not touched.\r\n- **Patterns** in `.gitignore`:\r\n  - `*.log`: the star `*` means \"any name\". It ignores `debug.log`, `error.log` and so on.\r\n  - `node_modules/`: the slash at the end means \"this whole folder\".\r\n- `.gitignore` only affects **untracked** files. A file that is already committed stays tracked (see the Common Mistake below).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**Leaked secrets and *Amānah***\r\n\r\n- Security researchers scan public code websites every year. They find **millions** of passwords and secret keys that people committed by accident.\r\n- Automatic programs search for these leaks all the time. A leaked password can be misused within minutes.\r\n- In Islam, a secret someone trusts you with is an ***Amānah*** (a trust). A website's passwords and its users' data are exactly that.\r\n- So: write the `.gitignore` **before the first commit**. If a secret was ever committed, **change the password or key**. Deleting the file later is not enough, because old commits still contain it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou have a file named `database.log`. Your `.gitignore` contains `*.log`. You run `git add .` and then `git status`. Will `database.log` be listed under **Changes to be committed**?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**No.** `git add .` skips every file that matches a pattern in `.gitignore`. `database.log` does not appear anywhere in `git status`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Giant Repository**\r\n\r\nA student's project took 45 minutes to copy to a new computer. The lead teacher looked inside and found a `node_modules/` folder with 50,000 files in it.\r\n\r\n- **The clue:** `node_modules/` (for JavaScript) and `vendor/` (for PHP) hold downloaded libraries. They can be rebuilt any time with `npm install` or `composer install`.\r\n- **The fix:** add `node_modules/` and `vendor/` to `.gitignore`. Keep only your own code in Git.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Adding a secret file to `.gitignore` too late**\r\n\r\n❌ **Wrong:** you commit `.env` first, and only afterwards add `.env` to `.gitignore`.\r\n\r\n**What happens:** nothing changes. Git keeps tracking `.env`, because it is already part of a commit. `git status` does not complain, so the mistake is easy to miss:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\t.gitignore\r\n\r\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present (use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\n✅ **Fixed version:** tell Git to stop tracking the file, but keep it on your computer:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit rm --cached .env\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nrm '.env'\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tdeleted:    .env\r\n\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\t.gitignore\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen commit with `git add .gitignore` and `git commit -m \"Stop tracking .env\"`. The file stays on your disk, but the next commits will not include it. The **old** commit still contains the secret, so change that password too.\r\n\r\n**Naming the file `gitignore.txt`** is the other common slip. Windows may hide the `.txt` ending. The name must be exactly `.gitignore`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository, create `notes.txt`, `test.log` and `.env` (with the line `KEY=practice`).\r\n2. Run `git status` and count the untracked files.\r\n3. Create a `.gitignore` that hides `test.log` and `.env`.\r\n4. Run `git status` again. Which files are left?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nYour `.gitignore` needs two lines: `*.log` and `.env`. At the end, only `.gitignore` and `notes.txt` should be listed as untracked.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git add <file>` stages one file; `git add .` stages everything not ignored.\r\n- `git restore --staged <file>` unstages a file and keeps your edits.\r\n- `.gitignore` lists files and folders Git must skip, such as `.env`, `*.log` and `node_modules/`.\r\n- Write `.gitignore` before your first commit. It does not affect files that are already tracked.\r\n- If a secret was committed, use `git rm --cached` and **change the secret**. This is *Amānah*.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which command stages all new and changed files in the current folder?\r\n2. What is the exact name of the file that tells Git what to ignore?\r\n3. How do you unstage a file without losing its edits?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git add .`\r\n2. `.gitignore`\r\n3. `git restore --staged <file>` (before the very first commit, use `git rm --cached <file>`).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why should library folders like `node_modules/` or `vendor/` stay out of Git?\r\n2. Explain how `.gitignore` works as a safety shield for a website.\r\n3. Write a `.gitignore` for a PHP and MySQL school project. It must ignore `.env`, the `vendor/` folder and every database backup ending in `.sql`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. They are very large and can be downloaded again with `npm install` or `composer install`. Keeping them out makes the repository small and fast.\r\n2. Once a file name or pattern is listed, `git add .` skips it every time. You cannot accidentally commit a password file, even when you are in a hurry.\r\n3. One possible file:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n# Secret settings\r\n.env\r\n\r\n# Libraries from Composer\r\nvendor/\r\n\r\n# Database backups\r\n*.sql\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n**The leaked key.** Three commits ago you committed `config.env` with a real secret key in it.\r\n\r\n1. Is adding `config.env` to `.gitignore` now enough to protect the key? Explain why.\r\n2. Write the commands that stop Git tracking the file but keep it on your computer.\r\n3. Say what else you must do to keep the trust (*Amānah*).\r\n4. Bonus: write a starter `.gitignore` for Junior Coders projects (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP and Laravel).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. No. `.gitignore` does not affect files that are already tracked. The key also stays inside the three old commits.\r\n2. Commands:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\necho \"config.env\" >> .gitignore\r\ngit rm --cached config.env\r\ngit add .gitignore\r\ngit commit -m \"Stop tracking config.env\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Treat the key as leaked. Create a new key and switch the old one off. Removing the file from Git does not remove it from the old commits.\r\n4. A starter template:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n# Secrets\r\n.env\r\n*.env\r\n\r\n# Libraries\r\nnode_modules/\r\nvendor/\r\n\r\n# Laravel generated files\r\nstorage/*.key\r\npublic/hot\r\n\r\n# Logs and backups\r\n*.log\r\n*.sql\r\n\r\n# Operating system and editor files\r\n.DS_Store\r\nThumbs.db\r\n.vscode/\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-essential-workflow/ch06-git-commit.md": "# Chapter 6: Crafting Meaningful Commits 📝\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Captain's Logbook**\r\n\r\nA ship's captain keeps a logbook of the whole voyage. A useless entry says: *\"Did some stuff. Moved the ship.\"* A helpful entry says: *\"08:00. Turned 15 degrees east to avoid strong monsoon winds.\"* Anyone reading it later knows what happened and why. In Git, **a commit message is your entry in the project's logbook**, and the next reader may be you, six months from now.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Save staged changes as a commit with `git commit -m`.\r\n- Write clear commit messages in the present tense (\"Add\", \"Fix\", \"Update\").\r\n- Use the **Conventional Commits** labels `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:` and `style:`.\r\n- Keep each commit to one idea (an **atomic commit**).\r\n- Fix your last commit with `git commit --amend`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Shape of a Good Commit Message\r\n\r\n```text\r\n TYPE  DESCRIPTION\r\n ┌──┐  ┌──────────────────────────┐\r\n feat: Add attendance table\r\n       └─ present tense verb first\r\n Keep the whole line under 72 characters.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Type** says what kind of change it is (see the table below).\r\n- **Description** says what the commit does, starting with a verb in the present tense.\r\n\r\n| Type | Use it for | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `feat:` | a new feature | `feat: Add student attendance table` |\r\n| `fix:` | a bug fix | `fix: Close the footer div on mobile` |\r\n| `docs:` | documentation only | `docs: Update setup steps in README` |\r\n| `style:` | look or formatting | `style: Improve button colour contrast` |\r\n| `refactor:` | tidier code, same result | `refactor: Simplify the grade loop` |\r\n\r\n### Bad vs. good messages\r\n\r\n| Bad message ❌ | Clear message ✅ |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `Update stuff` | `feat: Add student attendance table` |\r\n| `Fix bug` | `fix: Close the footer div on mobile` |\r\n| `asdf` | `docs: Update setup steps in README` |\r\n| `Finally done!!!` | `style: Improve button colour contrast` |\r\n\r\n**Present tense trick:** a good message completes the sentence *\"This commit will…\"*\r\n\r\n- *This commit will…* **Add student login form** ✅\r\n- *This commit will…* **Added student login form** ❌\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Making Commits\r\n\r\nThe Eco Club website already has a first commit. Now we add a navigation bar.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add nav.html style.css\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tnew file:   nav.html\r\n\tnew file:   style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth files are staged. Now save them as a commit:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add navigation bar with links\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main c3898ce] feat: Add navigation bar with links\r\n 2 files changed, 5 insertions(+)\r\n create mode 100644 nav.html\r\n create mode 100644 style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour commit ID (here `c3898ce`) will be different. The very first commit in a repository also says `(root-commit)`, like this: `[main (root-commit) b9a401f] Add HTML skeleton for club home page`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- `git commit` takes **only what is in the staging area** and saves it as a new snapshot. It records your name, email and the time.\r\n- `-m \"...\"` gives the message straight on the command line. Without `-m`, Git opens a text editor.\r\n- `[main c3898ce]` shows the branch and the short commit ID.\r\n- `2 files changed, 5 insertions(+)` counts the lines added (`+`) and removed (`-`).\r\n- After the commit, `git status` says `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🩹 Fixing the Last Commit with `--amend`\r\n\r\nYou spot a typo right after committing:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add navigation bar wiht links\"\r\ngit commit --amend -m \"feat: Add navigation bar with links\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main ce0aaf0] feat: Add navigation bar with links\r\n Date: Tue Oct 13 08:06:26 2026 +0530\r\n 2 files changed, 5 insertions(+)\r\n create mode 100644 nav.html\r\n create mode 100644 style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\n`--amend` **replaces** the last commit with a new one. Notice the ID changed (`ce0aaf0`). You can also use it to add a forgotten file:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add contact.html\r\ngit commit --amend --no-edit\r\n```\r\n\r\n`--no-edit` keeps the old message. Only amend commits that are still on your own computer. Once commits are shared with others (Chapter 14), changing them causes confusion for the team.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**Atomic commits**\r\n\r\n- In science, an atom was once thought to be the smallest piece that cannot be split.\r\n- An **atomic commit** holds **one** logical change and nothing else.\r\n- Fixed a login bug **and** changed the header colours? Make **two** commits.\r\n- If the new colours cause a problem tomorrow, the team can undo just that commit. The login fix stays safe.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nA student edits `readme.txt`, forgets `git add`, and runs:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"Update docs\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat does Git do?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\nGit refuses to make a commit and shows:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   readme.txt\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nA commit only saves what is in the staging area. Run `git add readme.txt` first.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Vague History**\r\n\r\nA new volunteer joins the school library website team and reads the log:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nc4f210a fixed it\r\n89b12a0 update\r\n1104e92 test\r\n33ba9f0 changed things\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **The problem:** nobody can tell what changed, which bug was fixed, or which commit broke the book search on Friday.\r\n- **The fix:** clear, respectful (*Adab*) messages such as `fix: Show results when the search box has spaces`. They save everyone hours of detective work.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Typing `git commit` without `-m`**\r\n\r\n❌ **Wrong:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit\r\n```\r\n\r\n**What happens:** Git opens a text editor inside the terminal. On many computers this is **Vim**, which feels like a trap for beginners. Typing letters does nothing you expect.\r\n\r\n✅ **Fixed version:** to escape Vim, press `Esc`, type `:q!` and press `Enter`. Git cancels and prints:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAborting commit due to empty commit message.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen commit the easy way:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"docs: Add contact details to README\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Past tense messages** (`Added footer`) are the other common slip. Write `Add footer`: the message describes what the commit **does**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository, create `index.html` with a heading and commit it.\r\n2. Add a `<p>` line to the file and commit again with a `feat:` message.\r\n3. Run `git log --oneline` to see both commits.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nThe pattern each time is: edit → `git add index.html` → `git commit -m \"feat: Add welcome paragraph\"`. `git log --oneline` shows one line per commit, newest first. You will learn more about `git log` in Chapter 7.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git commit -m \"message\"` saves the staging area as a new snapshot.\r\n- Nothing staged means nothing to commit.\r\n- Start messages with a present tense verb: \"Add\", \"Fix\", \"Update\".\r\n- Labels like `feat:`, `fix:` and `docs:` show the kind of change.\r\n- One idea per commit (atomic) makes undoing easy.\r\n- `git commit --amend` replaces the last commit; use it only before sharing.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which flag lets you type the commit message directly in the terminal?\r\n2. What is an atomic commit?\r\n3. Which command changes the message of the most recent commit?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `-m`, as in `git commit -m \"Add footer\"`.\r\n2. A commit that contains one logical change only.\r\n3. `git commit --amend -m \"New message\"`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why do Git teams write \"Fix bug\" instead of \"Fixed bug\"?\r\n2. How do clean commits show the Islamic value of excellence (*Iḥsān*)?\r\n3. Rewrite these messages in Conventional Commits style:\r\n   - *\"I changed the logo and color of footer\"*\r\n   - *\"Fixed that broken table thing on mobile\"*\r\n   - *\"Wrote contact us page\"*\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. The message describes what the commit **does** when it is applied, like an instruction. It reads like a label on the change: *\"This commit will fix bug.\"*\r\n2. *Iḥsān* means doing work carefully and beautifully, even the parts nobody praises. Clear commits are a gift to your teammates and to your future self.\r\n3. Possible answers. The first message mixes two ideas, so it becomes two commits:\r\n   - `style: Update club logo` and `style: Change footer colour`\r\n   - `fix: Make results table scroll on mobile`\r\n   - `feat: Add contact page`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n**Build a clean history.**\r\n\r\n1. Create a new repository called `bakery-site`.\r\n2. Make three atomic commits:\r\n   - Commit 1: an HTML skeleton.\r\n   - Commit 2: a navigation bar.\r\n   - Commit 3: a welcome banner.\r\n3. After commit 3, you notice you forgot `contact.html`. Add it **into** commit 3 without making a fourth commit.\r\n4. Print `git log --oneline` and check you still have exactly three commits.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir bakery-site\r\ncd bakery-site\r\ngit init\r\necho \"<h1>Halal Bakery</h1>\" > index.html\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add HTML skeleton\"\r\necho \"<nav>Home | Menu</nav>\" > nav.html\r\ngit add nav.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add navigation bar\"\r\necho \"<p>Fresh bread every morning</p>\" > banner.html\r\ngit add banner.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add welcome banner\"\r\necho \"<p>Contact us</p>\" > contact.html\r\ngit add contact.html\r\ngit commit --amend --no-edit\r\ngit log --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe log shows three lines, for example:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nded9177 (HEAD -> main) feat: Add welcome banner\r\n518e477 feat: Add navigation bar\r\nd3396d3 feat: Add HTML skeleton\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour IDs will be different.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-essential-workflow/ch07-git-log-and-diff.md": "# Chapter 7: Inspecting History & Differences (`log` & `diff`) 🔍\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Flight Recorder**\r\n\r\nEvery passenger plane carries a flight data recorder. It notes every change in height, speed and every pilot command during the flight. Afterwards, engineers can replay the whole journey instead of guessing. Git gives you two tools like this. **`git log`** is the project's diary of every commit, and **`git diff`** is a magnifying glass that shows exactly which lines changed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Read the project history with `git log` and the short form `git log --oneline`.\r\n- Filter the history by author, by file and by date.\r\n- See unstaged changes line by line with `git diff`.\r\n- See staged changes with `git diff --staged`.\r\n- Leave the `git log` screen with `q`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📜 Reading History with `git log`\r\n\r\nOur example is a school library website with three commits. Two people worked on it: Zainab and Omar.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\ncommit d6f001ce98483742cffa4ed7c0992c3d0663eac7 (HEAD -> main)\r\nAuthor: Omar Khan <omar@example.org>\r\nDate:   Tue Oct 13 08:06:46 2026 +0530\r\n\r\n    Update opening days\r\n\r\ncommit e80a3e7833258ea754aa313af019d8b4661ee7c1\r\nAuthor: Zainab Ahmed <zainab@example.org>\r\nDate:   Tue Oct 13 08:06:46 2026 +0530\r\n\r\n    Add heading colour\r\n\r\ncommit 2c23e920fccfecf7e270d3366c2aa4a3ec76fc5a\r\nAuthor: Zainab Ahmed <zainab@example.org>\r\nDate:   Tue Oct 13 08:06:45 2026 +0530\r\n\r\n    Add library home page\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe newest commit is at the top. Each one shows its full ID (the long **hash**), the author, the date and the message. `(HEAD -> main)` marks where you are now.\r\n\r\nThat is a lot of text. The short form shows one line per commit:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nd6f001c (HEAD -> main) Update opening days\r\ne80a3e7 Add heading colour\r\n2c23e92 Add library home page\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe 7-character code is the start of the hash. It is enough to point to a commit.\r\n\r\n### Filtering the history\r\n\r\n| Command | What it shows |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `git log --oneline -n 2` | only the newest 2 commits |\r\n| `git log --oneline --author=\"Omar\"` | only Omar's commits |\r\n| `git log --oneline -- style.css` | only commits that changed `style.css` |\r\n| `git log --oneline --since=\"7 days ago\"` | commits from the last week |\r\n| `git log --oneline --graph` | a drawing of the history (useful later for branches) |\r\n\r\nTry two of them:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline --author=\"Omar\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nd6f001c (HEAD -> main) Update opening days\r\n```\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline -- style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\ne80a3e7 Add heading colour\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `--` means \"what follows is a file name\". `--graph` draws a star `*` for each commit:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline --graph -n 2\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n* d6f001c (HEAD -> main) Update opening days\r\n* e80a3e7 Add heading colour\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith one straight line of commits the drawing is simple. In Chapter 10 you will see it split into branches.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔎 Seeing Changes with `git diff`\r\n\r\nNow Zainab changes the heading colour in `style.css` from `blue` to `teal`. She has **not** run `git add` yet.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit diff\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\ndiff --git a/style.css b/style.css\r\nindex ccf22a0..24cddd2 100644\r\n--- a/style.css\r\n+++ b/style.css\r\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@\r\n h1 {\r\n-  color: blue;\r\n+  color: teal;\r\n }\r\n```\r\n\r\nHow to read it:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n--- a/style.css   the old version\r\n+++ b/style.css   your new version\r\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@   lines 1 to 3\r\n-  color: blue;   removed (red)\r\n+  color: teal;   added (green)\r\n h1 {             unchanged (context)\r\n```\r\n\r\nA changed line shows up as one `-` line plus one `+` line.\r\n\r\n### Staged changes need `--staged`\r\n\r\nZainab now stages the file and runs `git diff` again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add style.css\r\ngit diff\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis prints **nothing**. Plain `git diff` compares your files with the **staging area**, and they now match. To see what is about to be committed, add `--staged`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit diff --staged\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\ndiff --git a/style.css b/style.css\r\nindex ccf22a0..24cddd2 100644\r\n--- a/style.css\r\n+++ b/style.css\r\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@\r\n h1 {\r\n-  color: blue;\r\n+  color: teal;\r\n }\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Command | Compares |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `git diff` | your files ↔ staging area (not yet staged) |\r\n| `git diff --staged` | staging area ↔ last commit (about to be committed) |\r\n| `git diff 2c23e92 d6f001c` | one commit ↔ another commit |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- When the output is longer than your screen, Git shows it in a **pager** (a viewer that shows one screen at a time). Press `Space` for the next page, `b` to go back, and **`q` to quit**.\r\n- `--oneline` shortens each commit to the short hash and the first line of the message.\r\n- In `git diff`, `-` lines were removed, `+` lines were added, and lines starting with a space are unchanged context, so you can see where the edit happened.\r\n- A good habit: run `git diff --staged` just before every `git commit`. You check exactly what you are about to save.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**Code reviews**\r\n\r\n- Before code goes into hospital systems or flight software, other engineers read it first. This is called a **code review**.\r\n- They read a diff: every red and green line.\r\n- They check that no password was left in, that no test messages like `console.log(\"here\")` remain, and that the code is careful and clear (*Iḥsān*).\r\n- On GitHub, reviews happen in **pull requests**, which you will meet in Chapter 17.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou change `color: blue` to `color: teal` in `style.css`. You have **not** run `git add`. You type `git diff`. Will you see `-  color: blue;` and `+  color: teal;`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Yes.** Plain `git diff` compares your files with the staging area. Your edit is not staged yet, so it shows up. If you ran `git add style.css` first, `git diff` would print nothing, and you would need `git diff --staged`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The Accidental Deletion**\r\n\r\nA student edited `script.js` and, without noticing, selected and deleted a 40-line function. Before committing, she ran `git diff`.\r\n\r\n- **The clue:** 40 red `-` lines scrolled past, all from a function she never meant to touch.\r\n- **The rescue:** she did not commit. She brought the file back to its last saved version with `git restore script.js`. You will learn `git restore` properly in Chapter 8.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Thinking the terminal froze inside `git log`**\r\n\r\n❌ **Wrong:** you run `git log` on a long project. The screen fills up, a `:` or `(END)` appears at the bottom, and typing new commands does nothing. You close the whole terminal window.\r\n\r\n**What happens:** you are inside the pager. It is waiting for keys like `Space` or `q`.\r\n\r\n✅ **Fixed version:** press **`q`**. You are back at your prompt. For short output, use `git log --oneline -n 5`.\r\n\r\n**Seeing nothing from `git diff`** is the other surprise. It does not mean your edits are lost. They are probably staged already, so run `git diff --staged`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository with at least one commit, edit `README.md` (add one line).\r\n2. Run `git diff` and find your new line with a `+`.\r\n3. Run `git add README.md`, then `git diff` (empty) and `git diff --staged` (your line again).\r\n4. Commit, then run `git log --oneline`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nIf `git diff` shows nothing in step 2, check that you saved the file in your editor. In step 4 your newest commit should be at the top with `(HEAD -> main)` next to it.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git log` shows every commit, newest first; `git log --oneline` shows one line each.\r\n- Filter with `--author`, `-- <file>`, `-n` and `--since`.\r\n- Press `q` to leave the `git log` pager.\r\n- `git diff` shows changes that are not staged; `git diff --staged` shows what will be committed.\r\n- `-` lines were removed, `+` lines were added.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which command shows the commit history?\r\n2. Which key quits the `git log` screen?\r\n3. What does a line starting with `+` mean in `git diff`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git log` (or `git log --oneline` for the short form).\r\n2. `q`\r\n3. A line that was added.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. What is the difference between `git diff` and `git diff --staged`?\r\n2. Why is it good practice to read the diff before you commit?\r\n3. Write the commands to: edit `README.md`, see the change, stage it, see the staged change, and commit it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git diff` shows changes you have **not** staged yet. `git diff --staged` shows changes that **are** staged and will go into the next commit.\r\n2. You catch mistakes before they become part of the history: a deleted function, a test message, or a password.\r\n3. Commands:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\necho \"Open Monday to Saturday.\" >> README.md\r\ngit diff\r\ngit add README.md\r\ngit diff --staged\r\ngit commit -m \"docs: Add opening days to README\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n**Become a history detective.**\r\n\r\n1. Show only the commits from the last 7 days, one line each.\r\n2. Show only the commits that changed `index.html`.\r\n3. Compare your oldest and newest commits with `git diff <old> <new>`.\r\n4. Make a shortcut so that typing `git lg` runs `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all`, then try it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline --since=\"7 days ago\"\r\ngit log --oneline -- index.html\r\ngit diff 2c23e92 d6f001c\r\ngit config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --decorate --all\"\r\ngit lg\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse the hashes from your own `git log --oneline`. An **alias** is a nickname for a longer command. In the library example, `git lg` prints:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n* fabef20 (HEAD -> main) Change heading colour to teal\r\n* d6f001c Update opening days\r\n* e80a3e7 Add heading colour\r\n* 2c23e92 Add library home page\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-undoing-and-stashing/ch08-undoing-changes.md": "# Chapter 8: Undoing Mistakes Safely (`restore`, `reset`, `revert`) 🛡️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Eraser, the Tray, and the Published Correction**\r\n\r\nYou write a science report in pencil, dislike a sentence, and rub it out with an eraser. Later you put the report in the teacher's tray, notice your roll number is missing, and simply take it back out. But once the report is printed in the school newspaper, you cannot secretly erase it from every copy. The honest way (*Ṣidq*, truthfulness) is to print a **correction notice** in the next issue. Git has a tool for each of these three moments.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Discard unsaved edits in a file with `git restore <file>`.\r\n- Take a file out of the staging area with `git restore --staged <file>`.\r\n- Undo a commit safely with `git revert`, which adds a new \"undo\" commit.\r\n- Compare `git reset --soft` and `git reset --hard`, and know why `--hard` is dangerous.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ Three Levels of Undo\r\n\r\nEach mistake lives in a different place, so each has its own tool:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWhere is the mistake?        Use this\r\n-----------------------      -------------------------\r\nEdited, not staged      ->   git restore <file>\r\nStaged (git add done)   ->   git restore --staged <file>\r\nAlready committed       ->   git revert <hash>\r\n```\r\n\r\nRemember the three trees from Chapter 2: the **working tree** (your files), the **staging area** (the box), and the **repository** (the saved commits).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example 1: Throw Away an Edit with `git restore`\r\n\r\nZainab's `index.html` was committed. She adds a silly test line and wants it gone. First she checks the status:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit even suggests the command. She runs it and checks again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe file is back to its last committed version. To restore **every** changed file at once, use `git restore .` (the dot means \"this whole folder\").\r\n\r\n> ⚠️ `git restore <file>` throws your edits away for good. They were never committed, so Git cannot bring them back.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example 2: Unstage a File with `git restore --staged`\r\n\r\nZainab ran `git add .` and accidentally staged `notes.txt`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tnew file:   notes.txt\r\n```\r\n\r\nShe takes it out of the box. Her file stays safe on disk:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore --staged notes.txt\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nUntracked files:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\r\n\tnotes.txt\r\n\r\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present (use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example 3: Undo a Commit with `git revert`\r\n\r\nZainab committed a banner with the wrong event date. She finds its **hash** (the commit's ID) with `git log --oneline`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\ndb01d89 Add donation banner\r\na6e7603 Add home page\r\n```\r\n\r\nShe reverts that one commit. The `--no-edit` option tells Git to use its ready-made message instead of opening an editor:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit revert --no-edit db01d89\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main 8f47f6b] Revert \"Add donation banner\"\r\n Date: Tue Oct 13 08:05:00 2026 +0530\r\n 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow look at the history:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n8f47f6b Revert \"Add donation banner\"\r\ndb01d89 Add donation banner\r\na6e7603 Add home page\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit did **not** delete `db01d89`. It added a new commit that does the exact opposite. The history stays honest: anyone can see what happened and when.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How `git reset` Is Different\r\n\r\n`git reset` moves your branch **backwards**, as if later commits never happened. `HEAD~1` means \"one commit before the current one\".\r\n\r\n| Command | Commit removed from history? | Your changes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `git reset --soft HEAD~1` | Yes | Kept, and still staged |\r\n| `git reset --hard HEAD~1` | Yes | **Deleted** from your files |\r\n\r\nAfter `git reset --soft HEAD~1`, the work is still waiting in the box:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n  (use \"git restore --staged <file>...\" to unstage)\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter `git reset --hard HEAD~1`, Git prints the commit you are now on, and the work is gone:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHEAD is now at 8f47f6b Revert \"Add donation banner\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Use `git revert` for commits other people may already have (you will share commits with others from Chapter 14).\r\n- Use `git reset --soft` only for your own commits that nobody else has seen yet.\r\n- Treat `git reset --hard` as a danger zone. It is one of the few Git commands that can destroy work for good.\r\n\r\n**A bit of history:** `git restore` arrived in Git 2.23 (2019). Before that, people used `git checkout -- <file>`, the older form you may still see online.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n**GitLab's 2017 database incident.** On 31 January 2017, an engineer at GitLab (a company that hosts code, like GitHub) deleted data from the wrong database server by mistake. It was a server command, not a Git command. Several of their backups turned out not to work, and about six hours of user data was lost. GitLab shared the whole recovery live and then published an honest report of what went wrong. Owning a mistake in public, instead of hiding it, is the same spirit as `git revert`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou have a brand-new file `secret_notes.txt` that you have **never** added or committed. You run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore secret_notes.txt\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Question:** Does Git delete the file?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**No.** Git does not know this file, so it prints an error and changes nothing:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: pathspec 'secret_notes.txt' did not match any file(s) known to git\r\n```\r\n\r\n`git restore` only works on files Git already tracks.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Panic Reset\r\n\r\nOmar made a commit with a small typo. In a hurry he ran `git reset --hard HEAD~1`. The commit vanished, but so did 40 lines of new code he had **not** committed yet.\r\n\r\n- **What went wrong?** `--hard` resets your files too, and uncommitted lines have no saved copy.\r\n- **The safer fix:** `git revert <hash>` to undo the commit, or `git reset --soft HEAD~1` to keep the work. Even better: commit or stash (Chapter 9) your unfinished work before any reset.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Using `git reset --hard` while you have unsaved work.**\r\n\r\nWrong:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit reset --hard HEAD~1\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: Git prints `HEAD is now at ...`, and every uncommitted change in your files is wiped. There is no undo button for those lines.\r\n\r\nFixed version: keep your work and only undo the commit.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit reset --soft HEAD~1\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Reverting the wrong commit.**\r\n\r\nWrong: typing a hash from memory, such as `git revert a6e7603`, when you meant the banner commit.\r\n\r\nWhat happens: Git undoes your home page instead of the banner.\r\n\r\nFixed version: always look first, then copy the exact hash.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline -n 3\r\ngit revert --no-edit db01d89\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn a practice folder (not a real project):\r\n\r\n1. Create a repository with `git init`, add `index.html` with one line, and commit it.\r\n2. Add a silly line to `index.html`. Run `git status` and read the red `modified:` line.\r\n3. Throw the edit away and prove the file is clean again.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nGit prints the command for you in step 2: `git restore index.html`. Then run `git status` and look for `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git restore <file>` throws away edits that are not staged. They cannot come back.\r\n- `git restore --staged <file>` takes a file out of the box but keeps your work.\r\n- `git revert <hash>` undoes a commit by adding a new opposite commit. History stays honest.\r\n- `git reset --soft` removes a commit but keeps the changes staged.\r\n- `git reset --hard` removes the commit **and** your changes. Use it only when you are sure.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which modern command throws away unstaged changes in a file?\r\n2. Which command removes a file from the staging area without deleting its contents?\r\n3. What is the main difference between `git revert` and `git reset`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git restore <file>`\r\n2. `git restore --staged <file>`\r\n3. `git revert` adds a **new** commit that undoes an old one, so history is kept. `git reset` moves the branch **back**, so later commits disappear from the history.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why is `git revert` safe on a branch other people share, while `git reset` can cause trouble?\r\n2. How does `git restore` help you experiment without fear?\r\n3. Add a silly paragraph to `index.html`, see it in `git status`, then remove it with Git. Which two commands did you use?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Teammates may already have the old commits. `git revert` only adds a commit, so everyone's history still matches. `git reset` removes commits, so your history no longer matches theirs.\r\n2. You can try any change. If it does not work, one command brings back the last committed version.\r\n3. `git status` (shows `modified:   index.html`), then `git restore index.html`. A final `git status` shows `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\nBuild a clean \"audit trail\" (a record that shows every change honestly):\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository, commit a file `banner.html` with the message `Add science fair banner`.\r\n2. Pretend you found a mistake in the banner. Undo that commit **without** deleting it from history.\r\n3. Run `git log --oneline` and check that both commits are listed.\r\n4. Extra: explain in one sentence what happens to staged files after `git reset --soft HEAD~1` and after `git reset --hard HEAD~1`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add banner.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Add science fair banner\"\r\ngit log --oneline\r\ngit revert --no-edit <hash-of-banner-commit>\r\ngit log --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe log shows `Revert \"Add science fair banner\"` above `Add science fair banner`. Your hashes will be different.\r\n\r\nExtra: after `--soft`, the changes stay in your files and stay staged. After `--hard`, the changes are deleted from your files and from the staging area.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-undoing-and-stashing/ch09-git-stash.md": "# Chapter 9: Stashing Work in Progress (`git stash`) 🗄️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**An Urgent Interruption at Your Desk**\r\n\r\nYou are halfway through a model of the solar system on your desk. Pieces are everywhere. Your mother calls: \"Please help carry the shopping bags in from the car.\" You cannot finish in ten seconds, and you do not want the pieces knocked over. So you place the half-built model safely on a high shelf, help, and later bring it back down to carry on. `git stash` is that shelf for your code.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what the stash is and when to use it.\r\n- Put unfinished changes aside with `git stash push -m \"message\"`.\r\n- See saved stashes with `git stash list`.\r\n- Bring work back with `git stash pop` or `git stash apply`.\r\n- Remove stashes with `git stash drop` and `git stash clear`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Stash Shelf\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWorking tree: half-done code\r\n      |\r\n      v   git stash push\r\n[ Stash shelf: your work kept safe ]\r\n      |\r\n      v   working tree is clean:\r\n          fix the urgent problem\r\n      |\r\n      v   git stash pop\r\nWorking tree: half-done code is back\r\n```\r\n\r\nA **stash** is a saved bundle of uncommitted changes. It is not a commit on your branch. It sits on a separate shelf until you take it back.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: The Stash Workflow\r\n\r\nZainab is halfway through a search filter in `app.js`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   app.js\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nHer teacher asks for an urgent fix elsewhere. She shelves her work with a clear label:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash push -m \"Half-done search filter\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSaved working directory and index state On main: Half-done search filter\r\n```\r\n\r\nHer working tree is now clean:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nShe can see what is on the shelf:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash list\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nstash@{0}: On main: Half-done search filter\r\n```\r\n\r\nShe makes the urgent fix and commits it. Then she brings her search code back:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash pop\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nChanges not staged for commit:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\r\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\r\n\tmodified:   app.js\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\nDropped refs/stash@{0} (dc064b7cd6b2ee5e5300d71501b539a7d3b64ea9)\r\n```\r\n\r\n`app.js` is modified again, exactly as she left it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 The Stash Commands\r\n\r\n| Command | What it does |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `git stash` | Shelves changes to tracked files, with an automatic label |\r\n| `git stash push -m \"msg\"` | Same, with your own label |\r\n| `git stash -u` | Also shelves **untracked** (brand-new) files |\r\n| `git stash list` | Shows all stashes: `stash@{0}` is the newest |\r\n| `git stash pop` | Brings the newest stash back **and** removes it from the shelf |\r\n| `git stash apply` | Brings it back but **keeps** a copy on the shelf |\r\n| `git stash drop` | Deletes one stash |\r\n| `git stash clear` | Deletes every stash |\r\n\r\nYou may see `git stash save \"msg\"` in older guides. It still works but is deprecated (no longer recommended). Use `git stash push -m \"msg\"` instead.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nDevelopers often jump between tasks: a new feature, a bug report, a review of a teammate's work. Git will not let you switch branches (Chapter 10) if your unsaved changes would be overwritten. Instead of making a messy commit such as `\"wip asdf\"`, they stash, switch, and pop the stash when they return.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou have one modified file. You run `git stash`, then `git status`.\r\n\r\n**Question:** What does `git status` print?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour change is safe on the stash shelf, so the working tree is clean.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Lost Draft\r\n\r\nBilal had 300 lines of half-written code in `planner.js`. A teammate needed his help right away. Bilal did not know about the stash, so he ran `git restore planner.js` to clear his screen.\r\n\r\n- **The problem:** `git restore` throws away uncommitted work. All 300 lines were gone for good.\r\n- **The lesson:** to clear your workspace **for a while**, use `git stash`. Use `git restore` only for changes you truly want to throw away.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Expecting a brand-new file to be stashed.**\r\n\r\nWrong: you create `help.html` (never added to Git) and run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: if that new file is your only change, Git saves nothing:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNo local changes to save\r\n```\r\n\r\nFixed version: add `-u` (include untracked files).\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash -u\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Using the stash as long-term storage.** Stashes have no branch and are easy to forget. Use them for minutes or hours. If the work matters for days, commit it on its own branch (Chapter 10).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn a practice repository with at least one commit:\r\n\r\n1. Edit a tracked file. Run `git status`.\r\n2. Stash the change with the label `\"testing stash\"`.\r\n3. Prove the working tree is clean, then bring the change back.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nUse `git stash push -m \"testing stash\"`, then `git status`, then `git stash pop`. Run `git stash list` at the end: it should be empty.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git stash` puts unfinished changes on a shelf and cleans your working tree.\r\n- Label stashes with `git stash push -m \"message\"`.\r\n- `git stash pop` brings work back and removes the stash; `apply` keeps a copy.\r\n- New, untracked files need `git stash -u`.\r\n- The stash is for short breaks, not long-term storage.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which command shelves unfinished changes and cleans your working tree?\r\n2. Which command lists all stashes?\r\n3. What is the difference between `git stash pop` and `git stash apply`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git stash` (or `git stash push -m \"message\"` to add a label).\r\n2. `git stash list`\r\n3. `pop` brings the changes back **and** removes the stash. `apply` brings them back but **keeps** the stash on the shelf.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why is stashing cleaner than committing broken code with a message like `\"wip broken\"`?\r\n2. Which option must you add to stash brand-new untracked files?\r\n3. Edit `index.html` and also create a new file `about.html`. Write the commands to stash both with the label `testing stash`, check the tree is clean, and bring both back.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A commit stays in the history forever. A broken \"wip\" commit makes the history confusing. A stash keeps the history clean.\r\n2. `-u` (include untracked).\r\n3. ```bash\r\n   git stash push -u -m \"testing stash\"\r\n   git status\r\n   git stash pop\r\n   ```\r\n   The middle `git status` shows `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n1. Can popping a stash cause a conflict? Test it in a practice repository:\r\n   1. Commit `style.txt` containing `color = \"blue\"`.\r\n   2. Change it to `color = \"green\"` and stash it.\r\n   3. Change it to `color = \"red\"` and commit.\r\n   4. Run `git stash pop`. What does Git print? Is the stash still in `git stash list`?\r\n2. Extra: write a three-line \"cheat card\" for younger students: when to commit, when to stash, when to throw away.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Yes. Both the stash and the new commit changed the same line, so Git reports a conflict:\r\n\r\n   ```text\r\n   Auto-merging style.txt\r\n   CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in style.txt\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n   Git also prints `The stash entry is kept in case you need it again.`, so `git stash list` still shows it. You fix the file the way you will learn in Chapter 12, then remove the stash with `git stash drop`.\r\n\r\n2. Cheat card:\r\n   - **Commit** when a small piece of work is finished and working.\r\n   - **Stash** when you must stop for a short while and come back later.\r\n   - **Restore** (throw away) only when you are sure you never want those changes.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-branching-and-merging/ch10-understanding-branches.md": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Branches (`git branch`, `switch`) 🌿\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Tree Trunk and Its Branches**\r\n\r\nLook at a great Chinar tree in a Srinagar garden. It has one strong trunk, and many branches grow from it. Birds may nest on one branch while new leaves open on another. If wind breaks a small branch, the trunk still stands. In Git, the trunk is your stable `main` branch, and each new idea grows on its own branch.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a branch is: a movable label that points to a commit.\r\n- List branches with `git branch` and see which one you are on.\r\n- Create and switch branches with `git switch -c` and `git switch`.\r\n- Explain what `HEAD` means.\r\n- Delete finished branches with `git branch -d`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Branching Timeline\r\n\r\n![Git Branching Lifecycle](../../assets/diagrams/git/git-branching-lifecycle.svg)\r\n\r\n```text\r\nmain:     C1 -- C2 ---------- C5 -- C6\r\n                 \\               /\r\nfeature:          C3 -- C4 -----'\r\n```\r\n\r\n`main` and `feature` grow at the same time without touching each other. Later, `C6` joins them together. That is a **merge**, which you will learn in Chapter 11.\r\n\r\n**HEAD** is Git's \"you are here\" marker. It points to the branch you are standing on right now.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Creating and Switching Branches\r\n\r\n**Step 1.** See which branch you are on. The star `*` marks it:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n* main\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 2.** Create a new branch **and** move onto it in one step. `-c` means \"create\":\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/dark-mode\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to a new branch 'feature/dark-mode'\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou will also see the older form `git checkout -b feature/dark-mode`. It does the same job.\r\n\r\n**Step 3.** Check again:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n* feature/dark-mode\r\n  main\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 4.** Add a file and commit it on the new branch:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add dark.css\r\ngit commit -m \"Add dark theme colours\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[feature/dark-mode 4e38327] Add dark theme colours\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n create mode 100644 dark.css\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 5.** Go back to `main` and list the files (`ls` from the terminal book; on CMD use `dir`):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\nls\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to branch 'main'\r\nindex.html\r\n```\r\n\r\n`dark.css` is not there. `main` is safe and unchanged.\r\n\r\n**Step 6.** Switch back to the feature branch:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch feature/dark-mode\r\nls\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to branch 'feature/dark-mode'\r\ndark.css\r\nindex.html\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour work is right back. Git changes the files in your folder to match the branch you switch to.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How Branches Work\r\n\r\n- **A branch is tiny.** Older tools copied the whole project to make a branch. In Git, a branch is just a small file in `.git/refs/heads/` that holds one commit hash (41 bytes). That is why creating one is almost instant, even in a big project.\r\n- **`git switch`** was added in Git 2.23 (2019). It only changes branches. The older `git checkout` did many different jobs, which confused people.\r\n- **`git branch -d <name>`** deletes a branch only if its work is already merged.\r\n- **`git branch -D <name>`** force-deletes a branch even if its work is not merged. Use it with care.\r\n\r\nIf you try `-d` on a branch that is not merged, Git protects you:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: the branch 'feature/dark-mode' is not fully merged\r\nhint: If you are sure you want to delete it, run 'git branch -D feature/dark-mode'\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nProfessional teams do not name branches `temp` or `stuff`. Many use a short prefix that says what the branch is for:\r\n\r\n- `feature/user-login`: a new feature.\r\n- `bugfix/mobile-overflow`: a bug fix.\r\n- `docs/update-readme`: documentation.\r\n- `refactor/clean-queries`: tidying code without changing what it does.\r\n\r\nThis is a common habit, not a rule built into Git. Your team decides.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou create a branch `experiment`, add five lines to `index.html`, and commit them there. Then you run `git switch main`.\r\n\r\n**Question:** Do you see those five lines in `index.html` while on `main`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**No.** Git changes your files to match the branch you switch to. The five lines only exist on `experiment`. Switch back to `experiment` and they return.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Missing Code Panic\r\n\r\nOn Monday, Maryam cried: \"Teacher, my contact form is gone!\" The teacher calmly ran `git branch`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n  feature/contact-form\r\n* main\r\n```\r\n\r\nMaryam had built the form on `feature/contact-form` on Saturday, but now she was standing on `main`. One command fixed it: `git switch feature/contact-form`. All her code was still there.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Committing on `main` by accident.**\r\n\r\nWrong: you start coding straight away, without checking where you are.\r\n\r\nWhat happens: your unfinished feature lands on `main`, the branch that should stay stable.\r\n\r\nFixed version: check first, then create your branch.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch\r\ngit switch -c feature/footer\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Deleting the branch you are standing on.**\r\n\r\nWrong:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch -d feature/dark-mode\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens (while on that branch):\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: cannot delete branch 'feature/dark-mode' used by worktree at 'C:/.../my-project'\r\n```\r\n\r\nFixed version: step off the branch first.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit branch -d feature/dark-mode\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn a practice repository with at least one commit:\r\n\r\n1. Create and switch to a branch called `feature/footer-redesign`.\r\n2. Add a file `footer.html` and commit it.\r\n3. Switch back to `main` and check whether `footer.html` is there.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\n`git switch -c feature/footer-redesign`, then `git add footer.html` and `git commit -m \"Add footer\"`, then `git switch main` and `ls`. The file should only appear on the feature branch.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A branch is a light label pointing at a commit. Making one is almost instant.\r\n- `git branch` lists branches; the `*` shows where you are (HEAD).\r\n- `git switch -c name` creates a branch and moves onto it; `git switch name` moves between branches.\r\n- Your files change to match the branch you switch to.\r\n- `git branch -d` deletes merged branches; `-D` forces it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which command lists all local branches?\r\n2. Which modern command creates a new branch and switches to it in one step?\r\n3. What does `HEAD` point to?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git branch`\r\n2. `git switch -c <name>` (older form: `git checkout -b <name>`).\r\n3. The branch (and so the commit) you are standing on right now.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why is a branch a safe place to build a new website feature?\r\n2. Why do people call Git branches \"lightweight\"?\r\n3. You are on `main`. Write the commands to create `feature/footer-redesign`, commit a change to `index.html` there, and return to `main`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Your experiments stay on the branch. `main` keeps working even if the feature breaks.\r\n2. A branch is only a tiny file holding one commit hash. Git does not copy your project.\r\n3. ```bash\r\n   git switch -c feature/footer-redesign\r\n   git add index.html\r\n   git commit -m \"Redesign footer\"\r\n   git switch main\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\n1. Make a practice repository with `index.html` and `style.css` on `main`.\r\n2. Create branch `design-a` and change the button colour in `style.css` to blue. Commit.\r\n3. Go back to `main`. Create branch `design-b` and make the button green. Commit.\r\n4. Switch between the two branches and refresh the page in your browser each time.\r\n5. Extra: on `design-b`, edit `style.css` but do **not** commit. Try `git switch design-a`. What happens, and how does `git stash` help?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c design-a\r\n# edit style.css: button { background: blue; }\r\ngit add style.css\r\ngit commit -m \"Make buttons blue\"\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit switch -c design-b\r\n# edit style.css: button { background: green; }\r\ngit add style.css\r\ngit commit -m \"Make buttons green\"\r\ngit switch design-a\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe browser shows blue buttons on `design-a` and green on `design-b`.\r\n\r\nExtra: Git refuses to switch because your edit would be overwritten:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by checkout:\r\n\tstyle.css\r\nPlease commit your changes or stash them before you switch branches.\r\nAborting\r\n```\r\n\r\nRun `git stash`, switch, and later come back and run `git stash pop`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-branching-and-merging/ch11-merging-branches.md": "# Chapter 11: Merging Branches (`git merge`) 🤝\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Two Mountain Streams Become One River**\r\n\r\nHigh in the Himalayas, two streams run down different valleys. One carries glacier meltwater; the other comes from a spring. Where the valleys meet, they join into one deeper river that flows on to the Jhelum. In Git, **merging** joins the work from a feature branch back into `main`, so it becomes part of the official project.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Merge a finished feature branch into `main` with `git merge`.\r\n- Tell a **fast-forward merge** from a **three-way merge**.\r\n- Follow the golden rule: stand on the branch that **receives** the work.\r\n- Delete branches after merging and check the result with `git log --graph`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ Two Kinds of Merge\r\n\r\n### Fast-forward merge\r\n\r\nIf `main` got **no** new commits while you worked, Git just slides the `main` label forward:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBefore:\r\nmain:      C1 -- C2\r\n                  \\\r\nfeature:           C3 -- C4\r\n\r\nAfter a fast-forward:\r\nmain:      C1 -- C2 -- C3 -- C4\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo new commit is needed.\r\n\r\n### Three-way merge\r\n\r\nIf `main` **also** got new commits, Git compares three snapshots:\r\n\r\n1. The **common ancestor**: the last commit both branches share (`C2`).\r\n2. The tip of `main` (`C5`).\r\n3. The tip of the feature branch (`C4`).\r\n\r\nThen it makes a new **merge commit** (`C6`) with two parents:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nmain:      C1 -- C2 -- C5 ----- C6\r\n                  \\            /\r\nfeature:           C3 -- C4 --'\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example 1: A Fast-Forward Merge\r\n\r\nZainab built a navigation bar on `feature/navbar`. Nothing new happened on `main` meanwhile.\r\n\r\n**Step 1.** Stand on the branch that receives the work:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to branch 'main'\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 2.** Run `git status` and make sure it says `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n\r\n**Step 3.** Merge the feature branch in:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit merge feature/navbar\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nUpdating dab6b86..a307ae2\r\nFast-forward\r\n index.html | 4 ++++\r\n 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 4.** The work is in `main`, so the old label can go:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch -d feature/navbar\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDeleted branch feature/navbar (was a307ae2).\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example 2: A Three-Way Merge\r\n\r\nThis time `main` moved on too. On `feat-a` Zainab added `page.html`. On `main` she added `other.html`. Then, standing on `main`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit merge --no-edit feat-a\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nMerge made by the 'ort' strategy.\r\n page.html | 1 +\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n create mode 100644 page.html\r\n```\r\n\r\n`--no-edit` accepts Git's ready-made message. Without it, Git opens a text editor so you can check the message. **ort** is the name of Git's default merge method.\r\n\r\nNow look at the picture of the history:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --graph --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n*   0fb1b5b Merge branch 'feat-a'\r\n|\\  \r\n| * a1d6b33 Add gallery page\r\n* | 10454ff Add about page\r\n|/  \r\n* a307ae2 Add navigation bar\r\n* dab6b86 Add home page\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe two lines split and join again at the merge commit.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 How It Works\r\n\r\n- **The golden rule:** stand where you want the code to **go**, then invite the branch to come to you.\r\n  - Want the feature in `main`? `git switch main`, then `git merge feature-name`.\r\n  - If you stand on the feature and run `git merge main`, you bring `main` into the feature instead.\r\n- **Fast-forward** creates no extra commit. Git only moves a label.\r\n- **`--no-ff`** (\"no fast-forward\") forces a merge commit even when a fast-forward was possible. Some teams like it because the graph then shows where each feature began and ended.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nMany teams use **continuous integration** (CI): when work is merged into `main`, a computer automatically runs tests. Some teams also publish the website automatically after the tests pass. So a broken merge into `main` can reach real users quickly. Merging only tested, working code is part of doing your work with excellence (*Iḥsān*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nBranch `nav` has two new commits. `main` has none. You run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit merge nav\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit prints `Fast-forward`.\r\n\r\n**Question:** Does Git create a new commit called `Merge branch 'nav'`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**No.** In a fast-forward, Git only moves the `main` label forward to the same commit that `nav` points to. No new commit is made.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Ghost Branches\r\n\r\nAhmad merged `feature/header` into `main` last week, but `git branch` still lists it:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n  feature/about\r\n  feature/footer\r\n  feature/header\r\n  feature/login\r\n* main\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **What happened?** Merging does **not** delete a branch.\r\n- **Good habit:** after merging, run `git branch -d feature/header`. If a branch is not merged yet, Git refuses, so you cannot lose work by accident.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Merging while standing on the wrong branch.**\r\n\r\nWrong (while on `feature/navbar`):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit merge main\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: `main` is merged **into your feature**. `main` itself does not get the navigation bar.\r\n\r\nFixed version:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit merge feature/navbar\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Deleting a branch before merging it.**\r\n\r\nWrong:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit branch -d feature/gallery\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: Git protects your work and refuses:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: the branch 'feature/gallery' is not fully merged\r\n```\r\n\r\nFixed version: merge first (`git switch main`, `git merge feature/gallery`), then delete with `-d`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nIn a practice repository:\r\n\r\n1. Commit a file on `main`.\r\n2. Create branch `feature-gallery`, add `gallery.html`, and commit.\r\n3. Merge it into `main` and delete the branch.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nAfter committing on `feature-gallery`, run `git switch main`, then `git merge feature-gallery`. You should see `Fast-forward`. Finish with `git branch -d feature-gallery`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git merge <branch>` brings that branch's work into the branch you are on.\r\n- Always switch to the receiving branch (usually `main`) first.\r\n- Fast-forward: Git just moves the label. Three-way: Git makes a merge commit.\r\n- Merging does not delete the branch; use `git branch -d` afterwards.\r\n- `git log --graph --oneline` draws the history so you can see the merge.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. Which branch should you be on before running `git merge feature-x` to add it to `main`?\r\n2. What is a fast-forward merge?\r\n3. Which command deletes a merged local branch?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `main` (the branch that receives the work).\r\n2. A merge where `main` had no new commits, so Git only moves the `main` label forward. No merge commit is made.\r\n3. `git branch -d <name>`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. When does Git do a three-way merge instead of a fast-forward?\r\n2. Why does a three-way merge need a merge commit?\r\n3. Write the commands to merge `feature-gallery` into `main` and then delete it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. When both branches have new commits since they split apart.\r\n2. The two lines of history must be joined. The merge commit has two parents and holds the combined snapshot.\r\n3. ```bash\r\n   git switch main\r\n   git merge feature-gallery\r\n   git branch -d feature-gallery\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\nCreate a true three-way merge:\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository, commit one file on `main`.\r\n2. Create branch `feat-a` and commit a new file `page.html`.\r\n3. Switch to `main` and commit a **different** new file `other.html`.\r\n4. Merge `feat-a` into `main` and run `git log --graph --oneline`.\r\n5. Extra: how does Git find the \"common ancestor\" of the two branches?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feat-a\r\ngit add page.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Add gallery page\"\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit add other.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Add about page\"\r\ngit merge --no-edit feat-a\r\ngit log --graph --oneline\r\n```\r\n\r\nGit prints `Merge made by the 'ort' strategy.` and the graph shows two lines joining at `Merge branch 'feat-a'`.\r\n\r\nExtra: every commit remembers its parent. Git walks back along both branches until it finds the newest commit they both share. That commit is the common ancestor.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-branching-and-merging/ch12-resolving-conflicts.md": "# Chapter 12: Resolving Merge Conflicts with Confidence 🕊️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Two Authors Edit the Same Line**\r\n\r\nYou and a classmate are writing the first line of an article for the school magazine. At home you write: \"The Srinagar Youth Tech Fest is held every October.\" At the same time your classmate writes: \"The Srinagar Youth Tech Fest takes place every April.\" A computer cannot know which month is right, and it should not silently throw away one person's work. So Git stops and asks the humans to decide. This is a **merge conflict**: not a crash, but Git protecting your work.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain why merge conflicts happen and why they are normal.\r\n- Read Git's conflict markers: `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>`.\r\n- Fix a conflict in your editor, then stage and commit the result.\r\n- Cancel a confusing merge safely with `git merge --abort`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ Anatomy of a Conflict\r\n\r\n![Merge Conflict Anatomy](../../assets/diagrams/git/merge-conflict-anatomy.svg)\r\n\r\nA conflict happens when **both** branches changed the **same lines** of the same file. Git writes both versions into the file between markers:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n<<<<<<< HEAD\r\n<h1>Welcome to Junior Coders Srinagar!</h1>\r\n=======\r\n<h1>Welcome to the Young Developers Club!</h1>\r\n>>>>>>> feature/title\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `<<<<<<< HEAD`: the start. Below it is **your** version (the branch you are on).\r\n- `=======`: the divider.\r\n- `>>>>>>> feature/title`: the end. Above it is the **incoming** version (the branch you are merging in).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Example: Resolving a Conflict Step by Step\r\n\r\nOn `main`, Zainab changed the title to include the city. On `feature/title`, she changed the same line to use the full club name.\r\n\r\n**Step 1.** Standing on `main`, she merges:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit merge feature/title\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAuto-merging index.html\r\nCONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in index.html\r\nAutomatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 2.** She checks the status:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYou have unmerged paths.\r\n  (fix conflicts and run \"git commit\")\r\n  (use \"git merge --abort\" to abort the merge)\r\n\r\nUnmerged paths:\r\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to mark resolution)\r\n\tboth modified:   index.html\r\n\r\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 3.** She opens `index.html` and sees the markers shown above. She has three choices:\r\n\r\n- Keep her version.\r\n- Keep the incoming version.\r\n- Combine both (very common).\r\n\r\nShe combines them and **deletes all three marker lines**:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h1>Welcome to Junior Coders Srinagar - Young Developers Club!</h1>\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 4.** She stages the file. This tells Git: \"I fixed this file.\"\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nAll conflicts fixed but you are still merging.\r\n  (use \"git commit\" to conclude merge)\r\n\r\nChanges to be committed:\r\n\tmodified:   index.html\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Step 5.** She finishes the merge with a commit:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -m \"Merge feature/title: combine both titles\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main c57f49e] Merge feature/title: combine both titles\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe history now shows both lines joined:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n*   c57f49e Merge feature/title: combine both titles\r\n|\\  \r\n| * bd2bf9d Use full club name\r\n* | 9ab822e Add city to title\r\n|/  \r\n* 0c0ae2a Add title\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 The Emergency Exit: `git merge --abort`\r\n\r\nIf a conflict feels too confusing, or you want to ask your teacher first, run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit merge --abort\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour project goes back to exactly how it was before you typed `git merge`. Nothing is lost. You can try again later.\r\n\r\nEditors such as Visual Studio Code show buttons above a conflict: **Accept Current Change**, **Accept Incoming Change** and **Accept Both Changes**. They simply edit the file for you. You still stage and commit afterwards.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nA conflict is a reminder to **talk** to your teammate. Good teams message each other kindly: \"Salaam! Our branches both changed the menu styles. Shall we look at them together for two minutes?\" Patience and good manners (*Adab*) turn a conflict into teamwork.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nA merge conflict happens in `style.css`. You fix the file and delete the marker lines.\r\n\r\n**Question:** What must you run before `git commit`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add style.css\r\n```\r\n\r\nStaging the fixed file tells Git the conflict is solved. Then `git commit` finishes the merge.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Broken Page\r\n\r\nHamza fixed a conflict in `app.js` and committed. When he ran the program, it crashed with a `SyntaxError` on line 24.\r\n\r\n- **The clue:** line 24 still said `<<<<<<< HEAD`. He forgot to delete a marker.\r\n- **The fix:** open `app.js`, delete every marker line, save, then `git add app.js` and commit the fix.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Leaving conflict markers in the file.**\r\n\r\nWrong: you pick a version but forget a marker, then stage and commit:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h1>Welcome to Junior Coders Srinagar!</h1>\r\n>>>>>>> feature/title\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: Git accepts it. Git does not check your code. The marker shows on your web page as strange text, or breaks a script.\r\n\r\nFixed version: search the file for `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>` before `git add`, and delete them all:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h1>Welcome to Junior Coders Srinagar!</h1>\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Panicking and deleting the whole project folder.**\r\n\r\nWhat happens: you lose the `.git` folder and all your history.\r\n\r\nFixed version: stay calm and run `git merge --abort`. Everything returns to how it was before the merge.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nMake a conflict on purpose, then fix it:\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository, commit `colour.txt` containing `color = \"blue\"` on `main`.\r\n2. Create `branch-a` and change the line to `color = \"green\"`. Commit.\r\n3. Switch to `main` and change the line to `color = \"red\"`. Commit.\r\n4. Run `git merge branch-a`, fix the file to `color = \"teal\"`, then stage and commit.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nAfter step 4's merge, `git status` shows `both modified:   colour.txt`. Open the file, replace everything with `color = \"teal\"`, then run `git add colour.txt` and `git commit -m \"Merge branch-a: use teal\"`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A conflict happens when two branches change the same lines.\r\n- Git marks both versions with `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>`.\r\n- Choose or combine the versions, and delete every marker line.\r\n- `git add` the fixed file, then `git commit` to finish the merge.\r\n- `git merge --abort` cancels the merge and returns you to safety.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n\r\n1. What does Git put into a file when a merge conflict happens?\r\n2. Which command cancels a merge with conflicts and goes back to safety?\r\n3. Which command tells Git you have finished fixing a conflicted file?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Conflict markers: `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>`, with both versions between them.\r\n2. `git merge --abort`\r\n3. `git add <file>`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n\r\n1. Why does Git refuse to guess which version to keep?\r\n2. What do `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `>>>>>>> branch-name` mark?\r\n3. After fixing `about.html`, which two commands finish the merge?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Only the people know which version is correct. Guessing could silently delete someone's work.\r\n2. `<<<<<<< HEAD` starts your version (the branch you are on). `>>>>>>> branch-name` ends the incoming version from the branch being merged.\r\n3. ```bash\r\n   git add about.html\r\n   git commit -m \"Merge feature: fix about page conflict\"\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\n\r\nWrite a \"Merge Conflict Survival Guide\" for younger students in your computer lab:\r\n\r\n1. Write five short numbered steps, from seeing the `CONFLICT` message to the final commit.\r\n2. Add one step that tells them what to do if they feel lost.\r\n3. Extra: explain why editors like Visual Studio Code show buttons such as **Accept Current Change** and **Accept Incoming Change**.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Stay calm. A conflict is normal, not an error.\r\n2. Run `git status` to see which files say `both modified`.\r\n3. Open each file and find `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>`.\r\n4. Keep the right lines (or combine them) and delete every marker.\r\n5. Run `git add <file>`, then `git commit`.\r\n\r\nFeeling lost? Run `git merge --abort` and ask your teacher or teammate.\r\n\r\nExtra: the buttons save time and avoid typing errors. They pick one side, or both, and remove the markers for you. You still check the result, then stage and commit.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-github-and-remotes/ch13-what-is-github.md": "# Chapter 13: What is GitHub? Cloud Repositories & SSH ☁️\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Central City Public Library**\r\n\r\nYou keep a notebook of your science ideas on your desk at home. It is safe, until your computer's disk stops working one day. Now imagine taking a copy to the city library. The library keeps a safe copy, and you send it your new pages whenever you write them. Trusted classmates can read your notebook there, report typos and suggest ideas. **GitHub is that library for code.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain the difference between **Git** (the tool on your computer) and **GitHub** (a website that stores repositories online).\r\n- Describe the difference between **public** and **private** repositories.\r\n- Compare the two ways to prove who you are to GitHub: **tokens** and **SSH keys**.\r\n- Generate an **SSH key** and add its public half to a GitHub account.\r\n- Follow the age rule and stay safe when you sign up for online services.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged **13 or older**. If you are younger, do not sign up on your own. You can still learn everything in Chapters 13–19: practise on your own computer with a \"pretend GitHub\" folder (Chapter 14 shows how), or use a classroom setup that your teacher manages.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ☁️ Git vs. GitHub: What's the Difference?\r\n\r\nA **remote repository** (or just **remote**) is a copy of your repository that lives somewhere else, usually on a server on the internet. GitHub is the most popular place to keep remotes.\r\n\r\n| Feature | Git ⚙️ | GitHub ☁️ |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **What is it?** | A program you run in the terminal | A website that hosts repositories |\r\n| **Where does it run?** | On your own computer | On GitHub's servers |\r\n| **Needs internet?** | **No.** Works fully offline | **Yes**, to send or receive work |\r\n| **Made by** | Linus Torvalds (2005) | Chris Wanstrath, PJ Hyett, Tom Preston-Werner and Scott Chacon (2008) |\r\n| **Main job** | Track versions and history | Back up code, share it, work in teams |\r\n\r\nGit is free, open-source software. GitHub is a company, owned by Microsoft since 2018. Other websites host Git repositories too, such as GitLab, Bitbucket and Codeberg.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔓 Public vs. Private Repositories\r\n\r\n- **Public repositories**\r\n  - Anyone can view the code and suggest improvements.\r\n  - Good for open-source tools, tutorials and student portfolios.\r\n- **Private repositories**\r\n  - Only you and the people you invite can see them.\r\n  - Good for unfinished work, or work that is not ready to share.\r\n\r\nEven in a private repository, never store passwords or secret keys (remember `.gitignore` from Chapter 5).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔑 Proving Who You Are: Tokens and SSH Keys\r\n\r\nSince **August 2021**, GitHub no longer accepts your website password when you push from the terminal. You need one of these instead:\r\n\r\n| Method | What it is | Good for |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Git Credential Manager** | A helper that opens a browser sign-in window and remembers you. It comes with Git for Windows. | The easiest start on Windows (HTTPS addresses) |\r\n| **Personal Access Token (PAT)** | A long secret code you create on GitHub and use instead of a password | HTTPS addresses, set by hand |\r\n| **SSH key** | A pair of matching keys: a **private key** that stays on your computer and a **public key** you give to GitHub | SSH addresses, no typing each time |\r\n\r\nThink of an SSH key like a padlock and its key. You give GitHub the **padlock** (public key). Only your computer has the **key** that opens it (private key).\r\n\r\n### Making an SSH key (Git Bash)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"zainab@example.org\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nPress **Enter** to accept the default file. You may type a **passphrase** (a password that protects the key) or press Enter twice for none. Git Bash then shows something like this:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nGenerating public/private ed25519 key pair.\r\nYour identification has been saved in /c/Users/zainab/.ssh/id_ed25519\r\nYour public key has been saved in /c/Users/zainab/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\r\nThe key fingerprint is:\r\n```\r\n\r\nTwo files now exist in the `.ssh` folder:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n📁 .ssh/\r\n   ├── id_ed25519       🔒 private: never share\r\n   └── id_ed25519.pub   🔓 public: give to GitHub\r\n```\r\n\r\nShow the public key and copy it:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ncat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\r\nclip < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA... zainab@example.org\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `clip` line copies the key to the clipboard on Windows. Your key's letters will be different.\r\n\r\n### Adding the key to GitHub (13+ only)\r\n\r\n1. Sign in to [github.com](https://github.com).\r\n2. Click your profile picture ➔ **Settings** ➔ **SSH and GPG keys**.\r\n3. Click **New SSH key**.\r\n4. Give it a title (for example *\"School Laptop\"*), paste the key and click **Add SSH key**.\r\n\r\n### Testing the connection\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nssh -T git@github.com\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first time, SSH asks if you trust GitHub's server. Type `yes`. Then you should see:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHi zainab-codes! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour computer and GitHub can now talk safely. 🎉\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection: The Open Source Revolution\r\n\r\n- GitHub reports well over **100 million developers** and hundreds of millions of repositories.\r\n- Well-known projects live there, such as the VS Code editor and the Python language (`python/cpython`).\r\n- Sharing useful, free tools on GitHub is a way to practise **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: knowledge that helps people in education, health and science.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou are on a train with no Wi-Fi. You write code and run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add .\r\ngit commit -m \"Add bus timetable page\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Question**: Will `git commit` work without internet?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Yes. Git works fully offline, and the commit is saved in your local repository. You only need internet later, when you send your commits to GitHub (Chapter 14).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Password Failure\r\n\r\nA student pushed to an HTTPS address and typed their GitHub website password. Git replied:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nremote: Support for password authentication was removed on August 13, 2021.\r\nfatal: Authentication failed for 'https://github.com/zainab-codes/eco-club.git/'\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Why?** GitHub stopped accepting website passwords in the terminal in 2021, to keep accounts safer.\r\n- **The fix:** sign in through Git Credential Manager's browser window, use a Personal Access Token, or set up an SSH key as shown above.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Sharing the private key instead of the public key.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** pasting the contents of `id_ed25519` (no `.pub`) into GitHub or an email.\r\n- **What happens:** GitHub refuses it as a key. Worse, anyone who sees it can pretend to be you.\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** only ever share the file ending in **`.pub`**. If your private key leaks, delete that key on GitHub and make a new pair.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Thinking Git and GitHub are the same thing.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** \"I can't use Git, because I don't have a GitHub account.\"\r\n- **What happens:** you skip learning a tool that works perfectly without any account.\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** Git runs on your computer for everyone. GitHub is one optional place to store a copy online.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nProve that Git does not need the internet:\r\n\r\n1. Turn off Wi-Fi (or unplug the network cable).\r\n2. In a practice repository, change a file, then run `git add .` and `git commit -m \"Test offline commit\"`.\r\n3. Run `git log --oneline` and check that your commit is at the top.\r\n4. Turn Wi-Fi back on.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nRun `git status` before and after the commit. Before, the file is listed as modified. After, Git says `nothing to commit, working tree clean`. No step needed the internet.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- **Git** is a program on your computer. **GitHub** is a website that stores copies of repositories.\r\n- A **remote** is a copy of your repository kept somewhere else.\r\n- Public repositories can be seen by anyone; private ones only by people you invite.\r\n- GitHub does not accept website passwords in the terminal. Use Git Credential Manager, a token or an SSH key.\r\n- Share only the **`.pub`** (public) key. Keep the private key secret.\r\n- GitHub accounts are for ages **13+**. Younger students practise locally or in a teacher-managed classroom.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Explain in one sentence the difference between Git and GitHub.\r\n2. Can you use Git on a desert island with no internet?\r\n3. Which SSH key file is safe to give GitHub: `id_ed25519` or `id_ed25519.pub`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Git is a program that tracks versions on your own computer; GitHub is a website that stores and shares Git repositories online.\r\n2. Yes. Every Git command for saving and viewing history (`add`, `commit`, `log`, `diff`, branches) works offline.\r\n3. `id_ed25519.pub`, the public key. The file without `.pub` is private and must never be shared.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why did GitHub stop accepting plain passwords in the terminal?\r\n5. What is the difference between a public and a private repository?\r\n6. Your school club wants to store (a) a free study-timer website for all students and (b) a draft quiz for next week's test. Which should be public and which private?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. Passwords are often reused and easy to steal. Tokens and SSH keys are long, random and can be removed one by one, so a leak does less damage.\r\n5. Anyone can view a public repository. Only the owner and invited people can view a private one.\r\n6. (a) The study timer can be **public**, so other students can use and improve it. (b) The quiz should be **private**, so nobody sees the questions early.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nGet your computer ready to talk to GitHub safely.\r\n\r\n1. Check for existing keys with `ls -la ~/.ssh` in Git Bash.\r\n2. If there is no `id_ed25519.pub`, make one with `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"you@example.org\"`.\r\n3. If you are 13 or older and have a GitHub account, add the public key and run `ssh -T git@github.com`. If you are younger, stop after step 2 and show your teacher the two key files.\r\n4. **Think:** your laptop is lost. How do you stop anyone using it to push to your repositories?\r\n5. **Write:** a short paragraph on how an honest GitHub portfolio (your own work, credited sources) shows real skill.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1–2. After `ssh-keygen`, `ls -la ~/.ssh` lists `id_ed25519` and `id_ed25519.pub`.\r\n\r\n3. A working test prints `Hi <your-username>! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.`\r\n\r\n4. On another computer, sign in to GitHub ➔ **Settings** ➔ **SSH and GPG keys** and **delete** the lost laptop's key. Also change your GitHub password. The key on the laptop is then useless.\r\n\r\n5. Example: *\"My GitHub portfolio shows the projects I really built, step by step, in my commit history. I credit any code or images I borrowed. This is honest (Ṣidq) and lets teachers see my true skills.\"*\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-github-and-remotes/ch14-push-and-remotes.md": "# Chapter 14: Connecting to Remotes & Pushing Code (`remote`, `push`) 🚀\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Sending Weather Data to the Science Centre**\r\n\r\nYour class builds a small weather station in the school garden. It records temperature all day and stores the readings inside. First, you set it up with the science centre's address. Then, at the end of the day, you press **Send** and the readings travel there. Now the whole community can see them. In Git, `git remote add` saves the address, and `git push` sends your commits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Connect a local repository to a remote with `git remote add`.\r\n- Check which remotes are set up with `git remote -v`.\r\n- Send commits to a remote with `git push -u origin main`, and later just `git push`.\r\n- Explain what `origin` and **upstream tracking** (`-u`) mean.\r\n- Practise the whole thing offline with a \"pretend GitHub\" folder.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Reminder:** GitHub accounts are for ages **13+**. Younger students can do every step in this chapter with the offline \"pretend GitHub\" folder below, or in a classroom setup run by a teacher.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📡 The Remote Connection Workflow\r\n\r\n![Git Remote Push & Pull](../../assets/diagrams/git/git-remote-workflow.svg)\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[ Your laptop ]           [ Remote ]\r\n branch: main             branch: main\r\n     │                         ▲\r\n     ├─ git remote add origin ─┤ address saved\r\n     │                         │\r\n     └─ git push -u origin ────┘ commits sent\r\n        main\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏠 Practise Offline: A \"Pretend GitHub\" Folder\r\n\r\nA **bare repository** is a repository with no working files, only the history. It is exactly what GitHub keeps on its servers. You can make one in a folder next to your project and use it as your remote. Everyone can do this, with no account and no internet.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n📁 practice/\r\n   ├── club-remote.git   (pretend GitHub)\r\n   └── eco-club/         (your project)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ncd practice\r\ngit init --bare club-remote.git\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nInitialized empty Git repository in C:/Users/zainab/practice/club-remote.git/\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour project `eco-club` already has two commits on `main`. Go into it and check its status:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ncd eco-club\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice there is no line about `origin` yet: the project does not know any remote.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔗 Linking and Pushing Your Project\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Save the remote's address\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add origin ../club-remote.git\r\ngit remote -v\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\norigin\t../club-remote.git (fetch)\r\norigin\t../club-remote.git (push)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Push your commits\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTo ../club-remote.git\r\n * [new branch]      main -> main\r\nbranch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Check status again\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow `git status` compares your branch with the remote copy too.\r\n\r\n### Pushing the next commit\r\n\r\nMake another commit, then look at the status:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit.\r\n  (use \"git push\" to publish your local commits)\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\n\"Ahead by 1 commit\" means the remote does not have your newest commit yet. Because of `-u`, a plain `git push` is enough now:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTo ../club-remote.git\r\n   65ca60a..b27eae1  main -> main\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe two short codes are the old and new commit hashes on the remote. Yours will be different.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🐙 The Same Steps with GitHub (13+)\r\n\r\n1. Open [github.com/new](https://github.com/new) and name the repository `eco-club`.\r\n2. Leave *\"Add a README\"*, *\".gitignore\"* and *\"license\"* **unticked**. Your local project already has its files.\r\n3. Click **Create repository** and copy its address.\r\n4. Run the same commands, using GitHub's address instead of the folder:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:zainab-codes/eco-club.git\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\nOver the internet, Git also prints progress lines like these (your numbers will differ):\r\n\r\n```text\r\nEnumerating objects: 10, done.\r\nCounting objects: 100% (10/10), done.\r\nDelta compression using up to 8 threads\r\nCompressing objects: 100% (6/6), done.\r\nWriting objects: 100% (10/10), 802 bytes | 267.00 KiB/s, done.\r\nTotal 10 (delta 2), reused 0 (delta 0), pack-reused 0 (from 0)\r\nTo github.com:zainab-codes/eco-club.git\r\n * [new branch]      main -> main\r\nbranch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.\r\n```\r\n\r\nRefresh the GitHub page: your files and full history are online. 🚀\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Breaking It Down\r\n\r\n- **`origin`** is a nickname for the remote's address. You could pick another name, but almost everyone uses `origin`.\r\n- **`git remote -v`**: `-v` means **verbose** (show more detail). It prints the address used to fetch (download) and to push (upload).\r\n- **`-u`** (long form `--set-upstream`) links your local `main` to `origin/main`. This link is called **upstream tracking**.\r\n  - You need `-u` only on the first push of a branch.\r\n  - After that, `git push` alone knows where to go.\r\n- **`origin/main`** is Git's note of where `main` was on the remote the last time you talked to it.\r\n\r\n### Pushing a feature branch\r\n\r\nBranches can be pushed too, so a teammate can see them:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/footer\r\ngit push -u origin feature/footer\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to a new branch 'feature/footer'\r\nTo ../club-remote.git\r\n * [new branch]      feature/footer -> feature/footer\r\nbranch 'feature/footer' set up to track 'origin/feature/footer'.\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection: Distributed Backups\r\n\r\nGit is a **distributed** version control system: every copy holds the full history.\r\n\r\n- Your laptop has the complete history.\r\n- The remote has the complete history.\r\n- If your laptop breaks, you lose nothing that you pushed. On a new computer, you download the project and its whole history again (Chapter 16).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou pushed once with `git push -u origin main`. Then you make a new commit and type only:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Question**: Does Git know where to send it, or does it complain?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** It pushes without complaint. The `-u` flag linked your local `main` to `origin/main`, so Git remembers where it goes.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The \"Remote Origin Already Exists\" Error\r\n\r\nA student ran `git remote add origin ...` a second time. Git replied:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: remote origin already exists.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Why?** The project already had a remote called `origin`.\r\n- **Check it:** run `git remote -v` to see the saved address.\r\n- **Change it:** run `git remote set-url origin <new-address>`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Creating the GitHub repository with a README, then pushing an existing project.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** ticking *\"Add a README\"* on GitHub, then running `git push -u origin main` from your project.\r\n- **What happens:** the remote already has a commit your laptop does not have, so Git rejects the push:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n ! [rejected]        main -> main (fetch first)\r\nerror: failed to push some refs to 'github.com:zainab-codes/eco-club.git'\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** when you push a project that already exists, create the GitHub repository **empty**. (If it is too late, Chapter 15 shows how to pull first.)\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: A typo in the remote address.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** `git remote add origin ../club-remot.git` (missing \"e\").\r\n- **What happens:** `git push` fails because nothing exists at that address:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nfatal: '../club-remot.git' does not appear to be a git repository\r\nfatal: Could not read from remote repository.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** you do not need to start again. Correct the address:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote set-url origin ../club-remote.git\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nMake your own offline remote and push to it.\r\n\r\n1. In a `practice` folder, run `git init --bare club-remote.git`.\r\n2. Make a project folder next to it, run `git init`, add a `README.md` and commit it.\r\n3. Link it with `git remote add origin ../club-remote.git` and run `git push -u origin main`.\r\n4. Run `git status` and look for `up to date with 'origin/main'`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nThe `../` means \"go up one folder\". Your project and `club-remote.git` must sit side by side in `practice`. If `git push` says `src refspec main does not match any`, you have not made a commit yet.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **remote** is another copy of your repository; `origin` is its usual nickname.\r\n- `git remote add origin <address>` saves the address; `git remote -v` shows it.\r\n- `git push -u origin main` sends your commits and sets up tracking. After that, `git push` is enough.\r\n- `git status` tells you if you are **ahead** of the remote (commits not pushed yet).\r\n- A **bare repository** in another folder is a free, offline \"pretend GitHub\" for practice.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which command links a local repository to a remote address?\r\n2. What is the usual nickname for the main remote?\r\n3. What does `-u` set up in `git push -u origin main`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git remote add origin <address>`\r\n2. `origin`\r\n3. **Upstream tracking**: it links your local `main` to `origin/main`, so later a plain `git push` (and `git pull`) knows where to go.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why is a remote copy safer than keeping code only on one flash drive?\r\n5. What does `git remote -v` show?\r\n6. `git status` says `Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 2 commits.` What does it mean, and what should you run?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. A flash drive can be lost or broken, and then the work is gone. With a remote, the full history exists in two places, so one broken copy loses nothing that was pushed.\r\n5. Each remote's nickname and the address it fetches from and pushes to.\r\n6. You have 2 commits that the remote does not have yet. Run `git push` to send them.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nSet up a club repository with a remote and share a feature branch.\r\n\r\n1. Create a bare \"pretend GitHub\" `club-remote.git` (or, if you are 13+, an empty GitHub repository).\r\n2. Link your practice project to it and push `main` with `-u`.\r\n3. Create a branch `feature/footer`, commit a footer, and push the branch so a teammate could review it.\r\n4. Run `git branch -a` and find your branch on the remote.\r\n5. **Extra:** write a short `README.md` that introduces your project (no personal details like your address or phone number), commit and push it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit init --bare ../club-remote.git\r\ngit remote add origin ../club-remote.git\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\ngit switch -c feature/footer\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"Add footer with club name\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/footer\r\ngit branch -a\r\n```\r\n\r\n`git branch -a` lists local branches and remote ones, which start with `remotes/origin/`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n* feature/footer\r\n  main\r\n  remotes/origin/feature/footer\r\n  remotes/origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-github-and-remotes/ch15-pull-and-fetch.md": "# Chapter 15: Staying in Sync: Fetching & Pulling (`fetch`, `pull`) 🔄\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The Morning Noticeboard**\r\n\r\nEvery morning, teachers pin new notices on the school noticeboard. They might move the science fair or change the exam timetable. If you walk past without reading it, you may prepare for the wrong day. In a team project, teammates push new commits to the remote all the time. **`git pull`** is reading the noticeboard and copying the updates into your own project before you start work.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Download teammates' commits and update your files with `git pull`.\r\n- Explain the difference between `git fetch` and `git pull`.\r\n- Describe what a **remote-tracking branch** such as `origin/main` is.\r\n- Fix a **rejected push** by pulling first.\r\n- Use `git stash` to pull safely when you have unfinished changes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Reminder:** GitHub accounts are for ages **13+**. If you are younger, practise this chapter with the offline \"pretend GitHub\" folder from Chapter 14, or in a teacher-managed classroom setup.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📥 `git fetch` vs. `git pull`: Peek or Download Everything\r\n\r\n```text\r\ngit fetch  = download new commits\r\n             into origin/main only\r\n             (your files do not change)\r\n\r\ngit merge origin/main\r\n           = add those commits to\r\n             your own branch\r\n\r\ngit pull   = git fetch + git merge\r\n```\r\n\r\nA **remote-tracking branch** like `origin/main` is Git's memory of where `main` was on the remote. It only moves when you talk to the remote (`fetch`, `pull` or `push`).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👀 Watching a Fetch, Step by Step\r\n\r\nYour teammate Amina pushed a new page, `about.html`, to the remote. You have not talked to the remote yet:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\n\"Up to date\" here only means \"up to date with what Git last saw\". Git does not check the internet by itself. Now fetch:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit fetch\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFrom ../club-remote\r\n   b27eae1..b8fae50  main       -> origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is behind 'origin/main' by 1 commit, and can be fast-forwarded.\r\n  (use \"git pull\" to update your local branch)\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour files have **not** changed yet; `about.html` is not in the folder. You can look at the new commit first:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit log --oneline main..origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nb8fae50 Add about page\r\n```\r\n\r\n`main..origin/main` means \"commits on `origin/main` that are not on my `main`\".\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔄 Pulling the Updates\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit pull\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nUpdating b27eae1..b8fae50\r\nFast-forward\r\n about.html | 2 ++\r\n 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)\r\n create mode 100644 about.html\r\n```\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit status\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\n`about.html` is now in your folder. **Fast-forward** (Chapter 11) means you had no new commits of your own, so Git just moved your `main` forward.\r\n\r\n### A good start-of-day routine\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit pull\r\ngit switch -c feature/new-page\r\n```\r\n\r\nPull on `main` first, then start your new branch from the fresh `main`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection: \"Pull Before You Push\"\r\n\r\nMany teams teach new members one habit first:\r\n\r\n> **\"Pull before you start work, and pull before you push.\"**\r\n\r\nIt takes seconds. It means you build on your teammates' latest work, and it makes merge conflicts smaller and rarer.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nA teammate added a new image, `logo.svg`, and pushed it. You run `git fetch` (not `pull`).\r\n\r\n**Question**: Does `logo.svg` appear in your project folder now?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** No, not yet. `git fetch` only updates `origin/main`; your files stay the same. `git status` will say your branch is *behind*. Run `git pull` (or `git merge origin/main`) and then `logo.svg` appears.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Rejected Push\r\n\r\nZainab committed a change to `style.css` and ran `git push`. Git refused:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTo ../club-remote.git\r\n ! [rejected]        main -> main (fetch first)\r\nerror: failed to push some refs to '../club-remote.git'\r\nhint: Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not\r\nhint: have locally. This is usually caused by another repository pushing to\r\nhint: the same ref. If you want to integrate the remote changes, use\r\nhint: 'git pull' before pushing again.\r\nhint: See the 'Note about fast-forwards' in 'git push --help' for details.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **What happened?** Amina pushed a commit while Zainab was working. Git will not let a push wipe out her work.\r\n- **The fix:** pull first, then push.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit pull\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFrom ../club-remote\r\n   b8fae50..4828937  main       -> origin/main\r\nMerge made by the 'ort' strategy.\r\n README.md | 1 +\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth people changed different files, so Git merged them by itself. (Git may open your editor to confirm the merge message; save and close it.) Now `git push` works. If you both changed the same lines, you would get a merge conflict to fix, exactly as in Chapter 12.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Pulling with unsaved changes in the same file.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** you are halfway through editing `index.html`, a teammate has also pushed a change to `index.html`, and you run `git pull`.\r\n- **What happens:** Git stops to protect your work:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:\r\n\tindex.html\r\nPlease commit your changes or stash them before you merge.\r\nAborting\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** put your changes away, pull, then bring them back (`git stash`, Chapter 9):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit stash\r\ngit pull\r\ngit stash pop\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSaved working directory and index state WIP on main: bfccb09 Add more lines\r\nUpdating bfccb09..1d4ccc5\r\nFast-forward\r\n index.html | 2 +-\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)\r\nAuto-merging index.html\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter `git stash pop`, `git status` shows `index.html` as modified again, now with your teammate's change included too.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Pulling while on the wrong branch.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** standing on `feature/profile` and running `git pull origin main`.\r\n- **What happens:** Git merges the remote `main` into `feature/profile`, which may not be what you wanted.\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** check the first line of `git status` before you pull. To update `main`, run `git switch main` first.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAct as two teammates on one computer.\r\n\r\n1. Use the `club-remote.git` and project from Chapter 14.\r\n2. Make a second copy for \"Amina\": in the `practice` folder run `git clone club-remote.git amina-copy`. (`git clone` copies a remote into a new folder; Chapter 16 explains it fully.)\r\n3. In `amina-copy`, add `about.html`, commit, and `git push`.\r\n4. In your own project, run `git fetch`, then `git status`, then `git pull`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nAfter `git fetch`, `git status` should say `behind 'origin/main' by 1 commit`. After `git pull`, `about.html` appears and the status says `up to date`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `origin/main` is Git's memory of the remote's `main`. It moves only when you fetch, pull or push.\r\n- `git fetch` downloads new commits but does not change your files.\r\n- `git pull` = `git fetch` + `git merge`: it downloads and updates your branch.\r\n- If the remote has commits you lack, `git push` is **rejected**. Pull first, then push.\r\n- Uncommitted changes in the same file block a pull. Use `git stash`, `git pull`, `git stash pop`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which command downloads and merges updates from a remote?\r\n2. Which two commands does `git pull` combine?\r\n3. What does Git do if you push while your branch is behind the remote?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git pull`\r\n2. `git fetch` and `git merge`\r\n3. It **rejects** the push (`! [rejected] ... (fetch first)`) and tells you to pull first.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why is `git fetch` safer than `git pull` when you just want to look at a teammate's changes?\r\n5. Why is pulling at the start of a work session good team manners?\r\n6. You run `git fetch` and `git status` says `behind 'origin/main' by 3 commits`. Which command lists those 3 commits?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. `git fetch` never changes your files or branch. You can inspect the new commits with `git log` first, and merge only when you are ready.\r\n5. You start from everyone's latest work, so you do not redo or undo a teammate's changes, and conflicts stay small.\r\n6. `git log --oneline main..origin/main`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nHandle a busy team day.\r\n\r\n1. Using two copies of a project (your project and `amina-copy`), make Amina push a change to `README.md`.\r\n2. Without pulling, commit a change to `style.css` in your copy and try `git push`. Read the error.\r\n3. Fix it so both changes end up on the remote.\r\n4. **Think:** if `git pull` ever leaves you in a merge conflict you are not ready for, which command cancels it? (Chapter 12)\r\n5. **Write:** a four-step \"before you code\" checklist for your school coding club.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n2. The push is rejected with `! [rejected]        main -> main (fetch first)`.\r\n\r\n3. ```bash\r\n   git pull\r\n   git push\r\n   ```\r\n   `git pull` makes a merge commit joining both changes; `git push` then succeeds.\r\n\r\n4. `git merge --abort` puts things back as they were before the merge.\r\n\r\n5. Example checklist:\r\n   1. `git switch main`\r\n   2. `git pull`\r\n   3. `git switch -c feature/<what-you-will-do>`\r\n   4. `git status` to check you are on the new branch with a clean folder.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-github-and-remotes/ch16-cloning-and-forking.md": "# Chapter 16: Cloning & Forking Open Source Projects (`clone`, `fork`) 🌍\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Borrowing a Craftsman's Blueprint**\r\n\r\nYou visit a woodworker in Srinagar and admire the pattern carved on a walnut desk. You politely ask if you may study how it was made. The craftsman gives you a full copy of the plan: \"Take it home, learn from it, and build your own bookshelf.\" You did not take the workshop; you were given a copy to learn from and improve. In Git, **cloning** and **forking** are ways of getting such a copy.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Copy a whole repository and its history with `git clone <address>`.\r\n- List what a clone sets up for you: the folder, the full history and the `origin` remote.\r\n- Explain the difference between a **clone** and a **fork**.\r\n- Keep your fork up to date with an `upstream` remote.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Reminder:** forking needs a GitHub account, which is for ages **13+**. If you are younger, you can practise every command here offline with bare folders (shown below), or in a teacher-managed classroom setup.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧬 Clone vs. Fork\r\n\r\n- **Clone** (`git clone`): copies a repository from a remote **to your computer**. You get every file and every commit.\r\n- **Fork** (a GitHub button): copies someone's repository **into your GitHub account**. Forking is a GitHub feature, not a Git command.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n1. Original repo  (author/tree-map)\r\n        │  click \"Fork\" on GitHub\r\n        ▼\r\n2. Your fork      (you/tree-map)\r\n        │  git clone <your fork>\r\n        ▼\r\n3. Your laptop    (edit, commit, test)\r\n        │  git push origin <branch>\r\n        ▼\r\n4. Your fork on GitHub\r\n        │  open a Pull Request\r\n        ▼\r\n5. Author reviews and merges 🎉\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou open a **pull request** (Chapter 17) to ask the original author to take your change.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📥 Cloning a Repository\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit clone https://github.com/zainab-codes/tree-map.git\r\ncd tree-map\r\ngit log --oneline\r\ngit remote -v\r\n```\r\n\r\nTo practise offline, clone a bare folder instead (`git clone ../tree-map.git`). This is the real output from such a clone:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nCloning into 'tree-map'...\r\ndone.\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\n5627961 Add tree list page\r\n7872ddb Start tree map\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\norigin\thttps://github.com/zainab-codes/tree-map.git (fetch)\r\norigin\thttps://github.com/zainab-codes/tree-map.git (push)\r\n```\r\n\r\nFrom GitHub, Git also prints progress lines while downloading; your hashes and numbers will differ.\r\n\r\n### What `git clone` did for you\r\n\r\n1. Made a new folder named after the repository (`tree-map`).\r\n2. Created the hidden `.git` folder inside it.\r\n3. Downloaded **every commit** in the history.\r\n4. Switched to the default branch (`main`) and filled the folder with its files.\r\n5. Saved the address as the remote **`origin`**, with tracking already set up, so `git pull` and `git push` work straight away.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Keeping a Fork Up to Date with `upstream`\r\n\r\nAfter you fork, the original author keeps adding commits. Your fork does not get them by itself. Add the original project as a second remote. By custom it is called **`upstream`**:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/author/tree-map.git\r\ngit remote -v\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\norigin\thttps://github.com/zainab-codes/tree-map.git (fetch)\r\norigin\thttps://github.com/zainab-codes/tree-map.git (push)\r\nupstream\thttps://github.com/author/tree-map.git (fetch)\r\nupstream\thttps://github.com/author/tree-map.git (push)\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow fetch the author's new commits, merge them into your `main`, and push them to your fork:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit fetch upstream\r\ngit merge upstream/main\r\ngit push origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFrom ../tree-map\r\n * [new branch]      main       -> upstream/main\r\nUpdating 5627961..3b479e5\r\nFast-forward\r\n README.md | 1 +\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\nTo my-fork.git\r\n   5627961..3b479e5  main -> main\r\n```\r\n\r\n(This output is from the offline practice, so the addresses are folder names.)\r\n\r\n### Why can't you push straight to someone else's project?\r\n\r\nYou can only push to repositories where the owner has given you **write access**. Otherwise anyone could change anyone's website. For other people's projects you fork, push to your fork, and open a pull request.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection: The Spirit of Open Source\r\n\r\n- **Open-source** software is software whose code anyone may read, study and improve.\r\n- Linux runs most of the world's web servers, and Android is built on the Linux kernel.\r\n- Chromium is the open-source base of the Chrome, Edge and Brave browsers.\r\n- PHP and Python are open source, and so is Git itself.\r\n- Studying and improving shared code is a practical way to spread **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**. Always respect each project's licence and credit the authors.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou clone a weather project that the author has worked on for 3 years. You go into the folder and run `git log`.\r\n\r\n**Question**: Do you see only the latest version, or the whole 3-year history?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** The whole history. A clone downloads every commit, so you can look back at any version the author ever committed.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective: The Permission Denied Push\r\n\r\nA student cloned the Python language's repository, fixed a typo in the documentation, committed, and ran `git push origin main`. GitHub replied:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nremote: Permission to python/cpython.git denied to zainab-codes.\r\nfatal: unable to access 'https://github.com/python/cpython.git/': The requested URL returned error: 403\r\n```\r\n\r\n- **Why?** The student does not have write access to that repository.\r\n- **The fix:** fork it on GitHub, clone the fork, push the fix to a branch on the fork, and open a pull request. (Big projects also have their own contribution rules; read their `CONTRIBUTING` file first.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Mistake 1: Cloning inside another repository.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** running `git clone ...` while you are still inside your own project folder.\r\n- **What happens:** you get a repository inside a repository. When you run `git add .`, Git warns you:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nwarning: adding embedded git repository: inner\r\nhint: You've added another git repository inside your current repository.\r\nhint: Clones of the outer repository will not contain the contents of\r\nhint: the embedded repository and will not know how to obtain it.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** run `cd ..` (or go to your general projects folder) **before** `git clone`. If it already happened, move the cloned folder out of your project.\r\n\r\n**Mistake 2: Downloading the ZIP instead of cloning.**\r\n\r\n- ❌ **Wrong:** clicking **Code ➔ Download ZIP** on GitHub to start working on a project.\r\n- **What happens:** you get only the files. There is no `.git` folder, so no history, no `origin` and no way to push.\r\n- ✅ **Fixed:** use `git clone <address>` to get the full repository.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nPretend to fork and clone, fully offline.\r\n\r\n1. In a `practice` folder, take your `club-remote.git` from Chapter 14 as the \"original\".\r\n2. Make your \"fork\": `git clone --bare club-remote.git my-fork.git` (`--bare` makes a copy with history only, like GitHub stores).\r\n3. Clone your fork to work on it: `git clone my-fork.git my-club`.\r\n4. Go into `my-club` and run `git log --oneline` and `git remote -v`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\n`git log --oneline` should show all the commits from the original. `git remote -v` shows `origin` pointing at the full path of `my-fork.git`. Next, try adding `upstream` with `git remote add upstream ../club-remote.git`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `git clone <address>` copies a repository and its **whole history** to your computer and sets up `origin`.\r\n- A **fork** is your own copy of someone's repository on GitHub. Forking is a GitHub feature, not a Git command.\r\n- You can only push to repositories where you have write access. For others: fork, push to your fork, open a pull request.\r\n- Add the original project as `upstream`, then `git fetch upstream` and `git merge upstream/main` to stay up to date.\r\n- Clone outside other repositories, and clone rather than downloading a ZIP.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which command downloads a full repository and its history?\r\n2. What is the difference between cloning and clicking \"Download ZIP\"?\r\n3. What is a fork?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. `git clone <address>`\r\n2. A clone includes the `.git` folder: the full history and a remote called `origin`. A ZIP has only the current files.\r\n3. Your own copy of someone else's repository, made inside your GitHub account with the **Fork** button.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why do contributors fork a project instead of pushing straight to it?\r\n5. What is the `upstream` remote for?\r\n6. You cloned a project. Which command proves that `origin` was set up for you?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. Most people do not have write access to other people's repositories, which keeps projects safe. A fork is a copy they can push to; from there they ask the owner to merge through a pull request.\r\n5. It points to the **original** project, so you can fetch the author's new commits and keep your fork up to date.\r\n6. `git remote -v`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nContribute to a project the safe way.\r\n\r\n1. Choose a project to practise on: your offline `club-remote.git`, or (13+) a beginner-friendly educational project on GitHub.\r\n2. Fork it (offline: `git clone --bare`), then clone your fork.\r\n3. Create a feature branch, make a small helpful change (for example, fix a typo in `README.md`), and commit it.\r\n4. Push the branch to your fork with `git push -u origin <branch>`.\r\n5. **Think:** the original project has 10 new commits since you forked. List the commands that bring your fork up to date.\r\n6. **Write:** a short paragraph on how open-source sharing reflects **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\nSteps 2–4 offline:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit clone --bare club-remote.git my-fork.git\r\ngit clone my-fork.git my-club\r\ncd my-club\r\ngit switch -c fix/readme-typo\r\ngit add README.md\r\ngit commit -m \"Fix spelling of 'environment' in README\"\r\ngit push -u origin fix/readme-typo\r\n```\r\n\r\nStep 5:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add upstream ../club-remote.git\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit fetch upstream\r\ngit merge upstream/main\r\ngit push origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\nStep 6 example: *\"Open-source developers share their code freely so others can learn and build useful tools. Every typo fixed or feature added helps people the authors will never meet. That is knowledge that benefits others, as Islam encourages.\"*\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-collaboration-and-pages/ch17-pull-requests.md": "# Chapter 17: Pull Requests & Polite Code Reviews 📬\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Submitting a proposal to the Student Council.**\r\nYou have an idea: a small weather garden in the school courtyard.\r\nYou do not start digging up the lawn on your own. That would be rude and confusing.\r\nInstead, you write a short proposal: what the garden is, why it helps, and which flowerbed it uses.\r\nThe Student Council reads it, praises the idea and asks for one change: \"Keep the watering pipe off the path.\"\r\nYou fix that, they approve, and the plan becomes part of the school. In software, this polite conversation is called a **Pull Request**.\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged 13 or older. If you are younger, practise the Git steps locally, or use a classroom account your teacher manages. Never sign up on your own.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what a **Pull Request** (PR) is and why teams use them.\r\n- Push a feature branch to GitHub and open a Pull Request.\r\n- Write a clear, friendly PR description.\r\n- Review a classmate's code with kindness and respect (*Adab*).\r\n- Update an open PR by pushing more commits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ The Pull Request Conversation\r\n\r\nA **Pull Request** is a request to merge your branch into another branch (usually `main`).\r\nIt is also a conversation page where teammates can read your changes and comment on them before they are merged.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[ your feature branch ]\r\n          │  git push\r\n          ▼\r\n[ GitHub: open Pull Request ]\r\n          │\r\n          ▼\r\n[ teammates read the changes ]\r\n  \"Lovely work! One typo on line 12.\"\r\n          │\r\n          ▼\r\n[ you push a small fix ]\r\n          │\r\n          ▼\r\n[ PR approved and merged into main ]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe name sounds odd at first. You are asking the project owner to **pull** your changes into their branch.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Step 1: Push Your Feature Branch\r\n\r\nYou already know how to make a branch (Chapter 10) and push it (Chapter 14).\r\nHere, Zainab adds a search box to a school's student directory site:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/student-search\r\ngit add search.js\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add instant name filter to student directory\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/student-search\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to a new branch 'feature/student-search'\r\n[feature/student-search 63b2c1c] feat: Add instant name filter to student directory\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n create mode 100644 search.js\r\nTo github.com:zainab-codes/school-directory.git\r\n * [new branch]      feature/student-search -> feature/student-search\r\nbranch 'feature/student-search' set up to track 'origin/feature/student-search'.\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour commit hash will be different. When you push to GitHub, it also prints a link that starts a pull request for this branch.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📬 Step 2: Open the Pull Request on GitHub\r\n\r\n1. Open your repository page on GitHub.\r\n2. A yellow banner appears: *\"feature/student-search had recent pushes\"*.\r\n3. Click **Compare & pull request**.\r\n4. Check the two branch boxes at the top: **base: main** ← **compare: feature/student-search**.\r\n5. Write a clear title and description, for example:\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n### What does this PR do?\r\nAdds a search box to the student directory.\r\nTeachers can type a name and the list filters at once.\r\n\r\n### How was it tested?\r\n- Tried it with 50 sample student names.\r\n- Checked that pressing Escape clears the box.\r\n\r\n### Screenshot\r\n(attach a picture of the new search box)\r\n```\r\n\r\n6. Click **Create pull request**.\r\n\r\nA teammate can now open the **Files changed** tab. There they see every added line in green and every removed line in red, just like `git diff` (Chapter 7).\r\n\r\n### Updating an open PR\r\n\r\nDid a reviewer ask for a change? You do **not** need a new PR.\r\nFix the file, commit, and push to the same branch again.\r\nGitHub adds the new commit to the open PR by itself.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🤝 The Manners of Code Review (*Adab*)\r\n\r\nWhen a teammate asks you to review their PR, they are trusting you. Be kind, clear and helpful.\r\n\r\n1. **Start with what is good.**\r\n   \"MashaAllah, this navbar code is very clean!\" or \"Great job checking empty form fields!\"\r\n2. **Talk about the code, never the person.**\r\n   - ❌ Unkind: \"Why did you write such bad CSS? It looks broken.\"\r\n   - ✅ Kind and useful: \"On small screens (under 400px) this card may overflow. What do you think about `flex-wrap: wrap` here?\"\r\n3. **Assume good intentions.**\r\n   Everyone is learning. Explain **why** you suggest a change.\r\n4. **Ask, do not order.** \"Could we...?\" invites a conversation. \"Change this.\" ends one.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nMany software teams never push straight to `main`.\r\nOn GitHub, a repository owner can turn on **branch protection rules**. These rules can require a Pull Request before any change reaches `main`. They can also require one or more teammates to click **Approve** first.\r\nMany teams also run automatic checks on every PR. These programs test the code before a person even reads it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYou have an open Pull Request for the branch `feature/navbar`.\r\nYou notice a small CSS mistake. You fix it, commit, and run `git push`.\r\n\r\n**Question:** Do you have to close the old PR and open a new one?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** No. GitHub sees the new commit on `feature/navbar` and adds it to the open PR. The **Files changed** tab updates for your reviewers.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The broken review tone.** A student wrote this on a classmate's PR:\r\n\r\n`\"Delete this entire file, it is completely wrong and makes no sense.\"`\r\n\r\n- **What is wrong?** It is harsh and does not help. It goes against good character (*Adab* and *Akhlāq*).\r\n- **A better comment:**\r\n  `\"Thank you for taking on this hard feature! I think the list might be stored twice here. Shall we sketch another way together at lab time?\"`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Using `git commit -am` for a brand-new file.**\r\n\r\nWrong:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/student-search\r\ngit commit -am \"feat: Add search\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: `-a` only stages files Git is **already tracking**. The new file `search.js` is left out. Git prints `nothing added to commit but untracked files present`, and your PR has no search code.\r\n\r\nFixed:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add search.js\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add search\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**2. One giant PR.**\r\nA PR with 2,000 changed lines across 30 files is very hard to review well. Keep each PR about **one** idea, like \"add search box\", so a reviewer can read it carefully.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nWrite a PR description (title plus three short parts) for this change: *\"I added a 'Back to top' button to the bottom of the library's book list page.\"* Use the template from Step 2.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nAnswer three questions: **What** does it do? **How** did you test it? What will the reviewer **see**? Start the title with `feat:`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A **Pull Request** asks to merge your branch into another branch, usually `main`.\r\n- First push your feature branch with `git push -u origin <branch>`, then click **Compare & pull request**.\r\n- A good PR description says what changed, how you tested it and what it looks like.\r\n- Pushing more commits to the same branch updates the open PR.\r\n- Review with *Adab*: praise first, talk about the code, explain why, ask instead of order.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is a Pull Request?\r\n2. Can you add more commits to a PR after it is opened?\r\n3. What does a reviewer do in a software team?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A request (and a conversation page on GitHub) to merge one branch into another, usually a feature branch into `main`.\r\n2. Yes. Commit and push to the same branch. GitHub adds the commits to the open PR.\r\n3. A reviewer reads the changes, checks they work and are clear, asks questions, suggests kind improvements, and approves when the code is ready.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why do many teams stop people pushing straight to `main`?\r\n5. How does a clear PR description save the reviewer time?\r\n6. Rewrite this review comment so it is kind and helpful: `\"This colour is ugly.\"`\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. `main` is the working version everyone shares. Sending all changes through PRs means another person checks each change first, so mistakes are caught before they reach everyone.\r\n5. The reviewer knows at once what the change is for, what to look at and how it was tested. They do not have to guess from the code alone.\r\n6. For example: `\"Nice layout! The light yellow text on white is hard to read. Could we try a darker colour so everyone can read it easily?\"`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nPractise the whole PR flow with a partner (or with your teacher's classroom account if you are under 13).\r\n\r\n1. In a practice repository on GitHub, create a branch `feature/footer` with `git switch -c`.\r\n2. Add a footer to `index.html`, stage it and commit with a clear message.\r\n3. Push the branch with `-u` and open a Pull Request with a title and a three-part description.\r\n4. Ask your partner to leave one kind comment. Fix what they suggest, commit and push again.\r\n5. Extra: your PR shows *\"This branch has conflicts that must be resolved\"*. What does it mean, and how do you fix it on your computer?\r\n6. Extra: write a 4-point \"Code Review Pledge\" for your school coding club, based on patience, kindness and encouragement.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\nSteps 1–4:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/footer\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add footer with school contact details\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/footer\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen click **Compare & pull request**, fill in the description and click **Create pull request**. After the review, edit the file, then `git add index.html`, `git commit -m \"fix: ...\"`, and `git push`. The PR updates by itself.\r\n\r\nStep 5: someone changed the same lines on `main` after you made your branch. Bring `main` into your branch and solve the conflict (Chapter 12):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit pull\r\ngit switch feature/footer\r\ngit merge main\r\n# edit the file: keep the right lines, remove the <<<<<<< ======= >>>>>>> markers\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"merge: Bring in latest main and resolve footer conflict\"\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\nStep 6, a sample pledge:\r\n1. I will start every review by thanking my classmate and naming something good.\r\n2. I will comment on the code, never on the person.\r\n3. I will explain **why** I suggest a change, and ask instead of order.\r\n4. I will be patient with beginners, because I was one too.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-collaboration-and-pages/ch18-issues-and-projects.md": "# Chapter 18: Issues, Milestones & GitHub Project Boards 📋\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**The science exhibition task board.**\r\nFour students are preparing a science exhibition stand. If they only rely on memory, someone forgets the poster boards and two people write the same summary.\r\nSo their teacher divides a whiteboard into three columns: **To Do**, **In Progress** and **Done**.\r\nEach task goes on a sticky note with one name on it, like \"Zainab: design the poster\" or \"Yasir: build the battery circuit\".\r\nEveryone can see who is doing what, and nothing is forgotten. GitHub gives software teams the same board through **Issues** and **Projects**.\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged 13 or older. If you are younger, plan tasks on paper or a class whiteboard, or use a classroom account your teacher manages. Never sign up on your own.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Create bug reports and feature requests as **GitHub Issues**.\r\n- Sort issues with **labels**, **assignees** and **milestones**.\r\n- Close an issue automatically with a keyword such as `fixes #12`.\r\n- Plan work on a three-column **GitHub Project** board.\r\n- Write a clear, helpful bug report.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📌 What Is an Issue?\r\n\r\nAn **Issue** is a page on GitHub for one task, bug or idea. Each issue gets a number, like `#12`.\r\nYou find them in the **Issues** tab of a repository. Anyone working on the project can comment on an issue.\r\n\r\nYou can give each issue extra details:\r\n\r\n| Detail | What it means | Example |\r\n|--------|---------------|---------|\r\n| Label | A coloured tag for the kind of task | `bug`, `enhancement`, `documentation` |\r\n| Assignee | The person doing the task | Zainab |\r\n| Milestone | A group of issues with a target date | \"Exhibition Day\" |\r\n\r\nGitHub gives every new repository some ready-made labels. They include `bug`, `documentation`, `enhancement`, `good first issue` and `help wanted`.\r\n\r\nA **milestone** is like a finish line. Put every issue needed for \"Exhibition Day\" in that milestone. GitHub then shows a progress bar of how many are closed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗂️ The Project Board\r\n\r\nA **GitHub Project** is a board for your issues. The column style is called a **Kanban** board (*kanban* is a Japanese word for \"signboard\"). Each card moves from left to right as the work moves forward.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n📋 TO DO\r\n  #14 Add a \"Back to top\" button\r\n      Yasir · enhancement\r\n⚡ IN PROGRESS\r\n  #12 Fix the mobile header\r\n      Zainab · bug\r\n✅ DONE\r\n  #10 Write the first README\r\n      Aisha · documentation\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn a phone the board is shown here as a list. On GitHub the three columns sit side by side.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Closing Issues from a Commit\r\n\r\nMention an issue number in a commit message and GitHub links the two together.\r\nUse a **closing keyword**, and GitHub also closes the issue:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -am \"fix: Stop mobile menu overflowing the screen (fixes #12)\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main 21056c5] fix: Stop mobile menu overflowing the screen (fixes #12)\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour commit hash will be different. Git itself does nothing special with `#12`. The magic happens on GitHub.\r\n\r\n**What happens on GitHub?**\r\n1. The commit appears inside Issue #12 as a link.\r\n2. When the commit reaches the **default branch** (usually `main`), GitHub closes Issue #12. This happens when you push it there, or when its Pull Request is merged.\r\n3. If the issue is on a Project board, the board's built-in workflow usually moves the card to **Done**.\r\n\r\n### Closing keywords GitHub understands\r\n\r\n- `close`, `closes`, `closed`\r\n- `fix`, `fixes`, `fixed`\r\n- `resolve`, `resolves`, `resolved`\r\n\r\nYou can also write them in a Pull Request description, for example `Closes #12`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🐛 Writing a Helpful Bug Report\r\n\r\nDo not just write \"The site is broken.\" Nobody can fix that. Give the steps, so others can see the bug for themselves:\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n### Bug description\r\nOn phone screens under 480px wide, the \"Submit Registration\"\r\nmessage opens behind the top menu.\r\n\r\n### Steps to see the bug\r\n1. Open index.html in a phone-sized browser window.\r\n2. Scroll to the bottom and tap \"Submit\".\r\n3. The green message is hidden behind the menu.\r\n\r\n### Where it happens\r\n- Phone, Chrome browser\r\n\r\n### What should happen\r\nThe message should appear in the middle of the screen,\r\nabove the menu.\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nMany software teams plan their work on a task board like this.\r\nSome teams hold a short daily meeting called a **stand-up**. Each person answers three questions:\r\n1. What did I finish yesterday?\r\n2. What will I work on today?\r\n3. Is anything blocking me?\r\n\r\nClear order and fair sharing of work reduce stress. They also show *Iḥsān*: doing every task with care.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nA developer writes:\r\n`git commit -m \"style: Update logo border (see #7)\"`\r\nThey wrote `see #7`, **not** `closes #7`. Then they push to `main`.\r\n\r\n**Question:** Will GitHub close Issue #7?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** No. GitHub links the commit inside Issue #7, so people can find it. But `see` is not a closing keyword, so the issue stays open. Only words like `closes #7` or `fixes #7` close it.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The abandoned feature.** A team built a neighbourhood clinic booking site. After three weeks, nobody knew what was finished, what was broken or what to do next.\r\n\r\n- **The cause:** they wrote code, but never wrote issues or made a board.\r\n- **The cure:** they spent 30 minutes writing 10 small issues. They added labels (`frontend`, `database`, `security`), gave each one an assignee and put them on a board. Now everyone knew the plan.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Starting work without assigning yourself.**\r\n\r\nWrong: you see issue #14 and start coding at once. A classmate does the same.\r\n\r\nWhat happens: two people build the same feature, and one person's work is wasted.\r\n\r\nFixed: assign the issue to yourself first (and move its card to **In Progress**). Then create your branch.\r\n\r\n**2. A keyword with the wrong number.**\r\n\r\nWrong: `git commit -m \"fix: Correct footer email (fixes #21)\"`, when the footer issue is really `#12`.\r\n\r\nWhat happens: when this reaches `main`, GitHub closes issue #21, a different task that is not done.\r\n\r\nFixed: check the issue number in the Issues tab before you commit. If a wrong issue closes, open it again with **Reopen issue**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOn paper (or in a text file), write a bug report for this problem: *\"On the school library site, the 'Search' button does nothing when you press Enter.\"* Use the four parts from the bug report example.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nThe steps should let a stranger see the bug in under a minute: which page to open, what to type, which key to press. Then say what happens and what *should* happen.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- An **Issue** is a numbered page for one task, bug or idea.\r\n- **Labels** say what kind of task it is, **assignees** say who does it, **milestones** group tasks under a target date.\r\n- A **Project** board moves cards from To Do to In Progress to Done.\r\n- `fixes #12` (or `closes`, `resolves`) closes Issue #12 when the commit reaches the default branch.\r\n- A good bug report gives clear steps, where it happens and what should happen.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. What is a GitHub Issue?\r\n2. Name three labels GitHub creates for a new repository.\r\n3. What would you write in a commit message to close Issue #5 when it is merged?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. A numbered page on GitHub for one task, bug or idea, where the team can discuss it.\r\n2. Any three of: `bug`, `documentation`, `duplicate`, `enhancement`, `good first issue`, `help wanted`, `invalid`, `question`, `wontfix`.\r\n3. A closing keyword with the number, for example `fixes #5`, `closes #5` or `resolves #5`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. How does a three-column board stop two students doing the same work?\r\n5. Why do clear \"steps to see the bug\" matter in a bug report?\r\n6. In your practice repository, create two issues: an `enhancement` called \"Add footer with contact email\" and a `documentation` issue called \"Add team members to README\".\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. Every task has one card with one name on it. Before starting, you can see whether someone else is already doing it.\r\n5. The person fixing the bug must first see it happen. Clear steps let them repeat it quickly, instead of guessing.\r\n6. Open the **Issues** tab, click **New issue**, type the title and a short description, then pick the label on the right side under **Labels**. Repeat for the second issue.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nClose an issue from your own commit, then plan a project.\r\n\r\n1. In your practice repository, note the number of the \"Add footer with contact email\" issue (say `#1`).\r\n2. Create a branch with `git switch -c feature/footer`.\r\n3. Add the footer, stage it, and commit with a message that closes the issue.\r\n4. Push the branch, open a Pull Request and merge it into `main`.\r\n5. Open the Issues tab and check that the issue is now closed.\r\n6. Extra: make a GitHub Project board for a community website, such as a \"Clean Water Awareness\" site for a local lake. Add six cards covering research, design, HTML, CSS, testing and publishing.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/footer\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add footer with contact email (closes #1)\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/footer\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen on GitHub: **Compare & pull request**, **Create pull request**, **Merge pull request**. When the merge lands on `main`, issue #1 shows **Closed** with a link to your commit.\r\n\r\nStep 6, sample cards:\r\n1. Research: collect three facts about keeping the lake clean.\r\n2. Design: sketch the home page on paper.\r\n3. HTML: build the home page structure.\r\n4. CSS: style the page for phones first.\r\n5. Testing: check every link and the page at phone width.\r\n6. Publishing: put the site live with GitHub Pages (Chapter 19).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-collaboration-and-pages/ch19-github-pages.md": "# Chapter 19: Publishing Live Websites with GitHub Pages 🌐\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\n**Opening your own public gallery.**\r\nImagine you painted watercolours of Dal Lake, Pari Mahal and the snowy Pir Panjal mountains.\r\nRight now they hang in your room, so only your family can see them.\r\nThen your principal offers you a glass-fronted gallery on the main road, free of charge, with your name above the door.\r\nNow anyone passing by can enjoy your work. For websites, **GitHub Pages** is that free public gallery.\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged 13 or older. If you are younger, open your website from your own computer for now, or let your teacher publish it with a classroom account. Never sign up on your own.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what GitHub Pages does.\r\n- Turn on GitHub Pages for a repository.\r\n- Read a GitHub Pages web address (URL).\r\n- Explain why the home page must be named `index.html`.\r\n- Update a live website with `git push`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 👁️ How GitHub Pages Works\r\n\r\n**GitHub Pages** is a free service from GitHub. It takes the HTML, CSS and JavaScript files in a repository and shows them as a real website.\r\nThese are **static** files: the server sends them exactly as they are. GitHub Pages cannot run server code like PHP or a MySQL database.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n1. You edit files on your computer\r\n   📁 lake-weather/\r\n      ├── index.html  ◀ home page\r\n      └── style.css\r\n\r\n2. You push to GitHub\r\n   git push\r\n\r\n3. GitHub Pages builds the site\r\n   reads main ➔ copies your files\r\n   ➔ turns on HTTPS\r\n\r\n4. The site is live\r\n   https://zainab-codes.github.io/\r\n           lake-weather/\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Reading the address\r\n\r\nFor a project repository the address looks like this:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nhttps://<username>.github.io/<repository>/\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo user `zainab-codes` with repository `lake-weather` gets `https://zainab-codes.github.io/lake-weather/`.\r\nThe `https` part means the connection is encrypted. GitHub sets this up for you on `github.io` addresses.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Turning On GitHub Pages\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Name your home page `index.html`\r\nYour repository's top folder needs a file named exactly `index.html`: all lowercase, no spaces.\r\nWhen a visitor opens your site address, GitHub Pages looks for this file first.\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Switch Pages on\r\n1. Open your repository on GitHub.\r\n2. Click the **Settings** tab (in the row of tabs at the top).\r\n3. In the left menu, under **Code and automation**, click **Pages**.\r\n4. Under **Build and deployment**:\r\n   - **Source:** choose `Deploy from a branch`.\r\n   - **Branch:** choose `main`.\r\n   - **Folder:** choose `/ (root)`.\r\n5. Click **Save**.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Visit your site\r\n- Wait a minute or two, then refresh the Pages settings screen. The first time can take up to about 10 minutes.\r\n- A message appears: *\"Your site is live at https://username.github.io/repo-name/\"*.\r\n- Click the link. Your website is on the internet!\r\n\r\nOn a free GitHub account, Pages works for **public** repositories. Anything in a public repository can be read by anyone, so never put passwords or private details in it (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔄 Updating the Live Site\r\n\r\nYou never upload files by hand. To change your website:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -am \"style: Change background to dark green\"\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter each push to `main`, GitHub Pages rebuilds the site. The new version is usually live within a minute or two.\r\nYou can follow each build in the repository's **Actions** tab.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\nMany teachers, students and open-source projects publish their websites with GitHub Pages.\r\nProject guides, class notes and student portfolios are common examples.\r\nIt is free for public repositories, so anyone with a computer and an account can share useful knowledge with the world.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nYour website is live on GitHub Pages.\r\nYou change the background to dark green and run:\r\n`git commit -am \"style: Change background to dark green\"`\r\nYou forget to run `git push`. Then you open your site's address.\r\n\r\n**Question:** Does the live site show the dark green background?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>👀 Click to check your answer</summary>\r\n\r\n**Answer:** No. The commit exists only on your computer. `git status` shows it:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOn branch main\r\nYour branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit.\r\n  (use \"git push\" to publish your local commits)\r\n\r\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\r\n```\r\n\r\nGitHub Pages only sees commits that are pushed to GitHub. Run `git push` and the site updates.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🕵️ Code Detective\r\n\r\n**The 404 mystery.** A student turned on GitHub Pages, but the address showed:\r\n`404 - There isn't a GitHub Pages site here.`\r\n\r\n- **The clue:** the repository's files were named `My Website.html` and `Style.CSS`.\r\n- **The cause:** there was no `index.html`, so GitHub Pages had no home page to show. (It can also use `index.md` or `README.md`, but this repository had neither.)\r\n- **The cure:** rename `My Website.html` to `index.html`, fix the CSS link to match the file name exactly, commit and push. The site appeared.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. A computer path for an image.**\r\n\r\nWrong:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<img src=\"C:/Users/Zainab/Desktop/lake.jpg\" alt=\"Dal Lake at sunrise\">\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens: on your own computer the picture shows. On the live site it is broken, because visitors' computers do not have your Desktop folder.\r\n\r\nFixed: put the picture inside the repository and use a **relative path** (a path that starts from the page's own folder):\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<img src=\"images/lake.jpg\" alt=\"Dal Lake at sunrise\">\r\n```\r\n\r\n**2. Wrong capital letters.**\r\n\r\nWrong: the file is `Images/Lake.JPG`, but the HTML says `images/lake.jpg`.\r\n\r\nWhat happens: Windows ignores the difference, so it works on your computer. GitHub Pages treats them as different names, so the live picture is missing.\r\n\r\nFixed: use lowercase names for all files and folders, and copy them exactly into your HTML.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nLook at one of your own HTML projects. Find every `src=\"...\"` and `href=\"...\"`.\r\nCheck two things: does any path start with `C:/` (or another drive letter)? Does every capital letter match the real file name?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Hint</summary>\r\n\r\nUse your editor's search (Ctrl + F) and search for `src=` and then `href=`. A safe path looks like `images/photo.jpg` or `style.css`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- **GitHub Pages** turns a repository of HTML, CSS and JavaScript into a free live website.\r\n- Turn it on in **Settings → Pages**: Deploy from a branch, `main`, `/ (root)`.\r\n- The address is `https://<username>.github.io/<repository>/`.\r\n- The home page must be `index.html`, and file names must match exactly, capitals included.\r\n- Every `git push` to `main` updates the live site. A commit that is not pushed does not.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\n1. Which free service shows a GitHub repository as a website?\r\n2. What exact name must the home page file have?\r\n3. What happens to your live site after you push new commits to `main`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. GitHub Pages.\r\n2. `index.html` (all lowercase).\r\n3. GitHub Pages rebuilds the site, and the new version is usually live within a minute or two.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\n4. Why does a path like `C:\\Users\\...` fail on the live site?\r\n5. What does the `https` in the address give your visitors?\r\n6. User `aisha-learns` publishes the repository `science-fair`. What is the site's address?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n4. That path points to a folder on **your** computer. Visitors' computers do not have it, and the GitHub server does not either. Use a relative path to a file inside the repository.\r\n5. The connection between the visitor and the site is encrypted, so nobody in between can read or change the page on the way.\r\n6. `https://aisha-learns.github.io/science-fair/`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nPublish one of your own websites (with a classroom account if you are under 13).\r\n\r\n1. Pick an HTML and CSS project from earlier in the course. Check that the home page is named `index.html`.\r\n2. Create a new public repository on GitHub and push your project to `main` (Chapter 14).\r\n3. Turn on GitHub Pages and open the live address.\r\n4. Change one heading, commit, push, and watch the live site update.\r\n5. Extra: can GitHub Pages run PHP code or a MySQL database? Explain why or why not.\r\n6. Extra: build a \"My Learning Launchpad\" page with a short bio and links to all your practice pages, and publish it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:zainab-codes/lake-weather.git\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen **Settings → Pages → Deploy from a branch → main → / (root) → Save**, and visit `https://zainab-codes.github.io/lake-weather/`.\r\n\r\nStep 4:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -am \"feat: Update home page heading\"\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\nStep 5: No. GitHub Pages only sends **static** files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images) to the browser. PHP and MySQL need a server that runs code for each visit, which GitHub Pages does not offer. JavaScript still works because it runs in the visitor's browser.\r\n\r\nStep 6: an `index.html` with a heading, a short paragraph about you (no private details like your home address or phone number), and a list of links such as `<a href=\"weather/index.html\">Lake weather page</a>`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-07-capstone-projects/proj01-solo-portfolio-repo.md": "# Project 1: Solo Portfolio Repository & GitHub Pages 🎨\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Project Overview\r\n\r\nIn this project you use everything from this book to build, save, document and publish your own **portfolio website**: a page that shows what you have learned and built.\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged 13 or older. If you are younger, do Steps 1–4 on your own computer (they need no account), and let your teacher publish Step 5 with a classroom account. Never sign up on your own.\r\n\r\n### What you will produce\r\n1. A Git repository on your computer, with your name and email set up.\r\n2. A `.gitignore` file that keeps system and editor files out.\r\n3. A home page, `index.html`.\r\n4. A `README.md` that explains the project.\r\n5. A clean history of at least 4 small commits with clear messages.\r\n6. A live website on GitHub Pages.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Chapters You Will Use\r\n\r\n- **Chapter 3:** setting your Git name and email.\r\n- **Chapters 4 and 5:** `git init`, staging and `.gitignore`.\r\n- **Chapters 6 and 7:** clear commit messages and `git log --oneline`.\r\n- **Chapter 14:** linking to GitHub and pushing.\r\n- **Chapter 19:** publishing with GitHub Pages.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Step-by-Step Guide\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Create the repository\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir my-portfolio\r\ncd my-portfolio\r\ngit init\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput (your folder path will be different):\r\n\r\n```text\r\nInitialized empty Git repository in C:/Users/you/my-portfolio/.git/\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Add a `.gitignore` first\r\n\r\nCreate a file named `.gitignore` with this content:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n.DS_Store\r\nThumbs.db\r\n*.log\r\n.vscode/\r\n.idea/\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese are system and editor files that do not belong in your project. Commit it:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add .gitignore\r\ngit commit -m \"chore: Ignore system and editor files\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[main (root-commit) f6a93e7] chore: Ignore system and editor files\r\n 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)\r\n create mode 100644 .gitignore\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour commit hashes will be different from the ones in this guide.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Build the home page\r\n\r\nCreate `index.html`. Replace \"Your Name\" with your first name. Do not add your home address, phone number or school name: a public site can be read by anyone (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n<head>\r\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\r\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\r\n  <title>Your Name | Junior Web Developer</title>\r\n  <style>\r\n    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, sans-serif; max-width: 800px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 0 20px; line-height: 1.6; color: #1e293b; }\r\n    header { border-bottom: 2px solid #f05032; padding-bottom: 20px; margin-bottom: 30px; }\r\n    h1 { color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 6px; }\r\n    .badge { background: #ffedd5; color: #c2410c; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; font-size: 0.85rem; font-weight: 700; }\r\n    .project-card { background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; }\r\n  </style>\r\n</head>\r\n<body>\r\n  <header>\r\n    <span class=\"badge\">Student Developer</span>\r\n    <h1>Your Name</h1>\r\n    <p>Learning to build helpful, honest websites for my community.</p>\r\n  </header>\r\n\r\n  <main>\r\n    <section>\r\n      <h2>My Projects</h2>\r\n      <div class=\"project-card\">\r\n        <h3>Lake Clean-Up Tracker</h3>\r\n        <p>A page that lists clean-up days and simple tips for keeping our lake clean.</p>\r\n      </div>\r\n      <div class=\"project-card\">\r\n        <h3>Class Library Catalogue</h3>\r\n        <p>A neat table of the books in our class library and who looks after them.</p>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </section>\r\n\r\n    <section>\r\n      <h2>Skills</h2>\r\n      <p>HTML &bull; CSS &bull; JavaScript &bull; Git &amp; GitHub</p>\r\n    </section>\r\n  </main>\r\n\r\n  <footer>\r\n    <p>Built with care while learning with Junior Coders.</p>\r\n  </footer>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nCommit it:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add portfolio home page\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Write the README\r\n\r\nCreate `README.md`. GitHub shows this file on your repository's front page.\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n# Your Name: Student Developer Portfolio\r\n\r\nMy portfolio website, built while learning HTML, CSS, JavaScript and Git.\r\n\r\nLive site: https://your-username.github.io/my-portfolio/\r\n\r\n## Skills\r\n- HTML, CSS and JavaScript\r\n- Git and GitHub, with clear commit messages\r\n\r\n## How I work\r\n- I build tools that help people and my community.\r\n- I keep private details out of public code (Amānah).\r\n- I write clean, tidy code (Iḥsān).\r\n\r\n## Contact\r\nPlease contact me through my teacher or open an issue in this repository.\r\n```\r\n\r\nCommit it:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add README.md\r\ngit commit -m \"docs: Add README with skills and contact\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 5: Push to GitHub and publish\r\n\r\nCreate an **empty** repository named `my-portfolio` on GitHub (no README, so the histories match). Then link and push. Replace `your-username` with your GitHub username:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:your-username/my-portfolio.git\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last lines of the output:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTo github.com:your-username/my-portfolio.git\r\n * [new branch]      main -> main\r\nbranch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow open **Settings → Pages**, choose **Deploy from a branch**, `main`, `/ (root)`, and click **Save**. After a minute or two your site is live at `https://your-username.github.io/my-portfolio/`.\r\n\r\n### Step 6: Make one more improvement\r\n\r\nAdd a third project card to `index.html`. Then:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add third project card\"\r\ngit push\r\n```\r\n\r\nRefresh your live site after a minute or two to see the new card.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧪 Project Checklist\r\n\r\n- [ ] `git log --oneline` shows at least 4 commits, each with a clear message.\r\n- [ ] `git status` says `nothing to commit, working tree clean`.\r\n- [ ] `git status` does not list `Thumbs.db`, `.DS_Store` or `.vscode/`, even if those files exist.\r\n- [ ] On GitHub, the repository front page shows your README.\r\n- [ ] The live GitHub Pages address opens your portfolio with `https`.\r\n- [ ] No private details (home address, phone number, passwords) appear in any file.\r\n\r\nAfter Step 4, `git log --oneline` looks like this (newest first):\r\n\r\n```text\r\na77e124 docs: Add README with skills and contact\r\ne3e275b feat: Add portfolio home page\r\nf6a93e7 chore: Ignore system and editor files\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Start every project with `git init` and a `.gitignore`.\r\n- Make one small commit for each step, with a clear message.\r\n- A `README.md` tells visitors what the project is.\r\n- `git push -u origin main` sends your work to GitHub, and GitHub Pages makes it a live site.\r\n- Every later `git push` updates the live site.\r\n",
  "part-07-capstone-projects/proj02-team-collaboration-lab.md": "# Project 2: Team Collaboration & Feature Branch Simulation 👥\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Project Overview\r\n\r\nIn this project you act out how a real two-person team works on one shared GitHub repository.\r\nWork with a lab partner, or play both roles yourself in two different folders.\r\n\r\n> **Age check:** GitHub accounts are only for people aged 13 or older. If you are younger, use a classroom account your teacher manages, or practise with a \"remote\" folder on your own computer (see the tip below). Never sign up on your own.\r\n\r\nYou will practise:\r\n1. Adding a teammate as a **collaborator** (a person allowed to push to the repository).\r\n2. Working only on feature branches, never straight on `main`.\r\n3. Opening and reviewing a **Pull Request** with kind, helpful comments (*Adab*).\r\n4. Bringing the team's latest work to your computer with `git pull`.\r\n5. (Optional) Making and solving a **merge conflict** on purpose.\r\n\r\n> **Tip: practise without GitHub.** `git init --bare shared.git` makes a \"bare\" repository: a folder that stores only Git history, with no working files. It acts like a GitHub repository on your own computer. Use its folder path wherever this guide says `git@github.com:...`. You cannot open a Pull Request there, so merge the branch yourself with `git merge`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Scenario and Roles\r\n\r\n- **Project:** *Srinagar Heritage Portal*, a small website about the city's historic places.\r\n- **Maryam (Developer A, team lead):** creates the repository, builds the home page and reviews Pull Requests.\r\n- **Bilal (Developer B):** clones the project, builds a \"Heritage Landmarks\" section on a branch and asks for a review.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💻 Step-by-Step Lab\r\n\r\n### Phase 1: Maryam sets up the project\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nmkdir heritage-portal\r\ncd heritage-portal\r\ngit init\r\necho \"<h1>Heritage Portal</h1>\" > index.html\r\ngit add .\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add homepage heading\"\r\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:maryam-codes/heritage-portal.git\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nInitialized empty Git repository in C:/Users/maryam/heritage-portal/.git/\r\n[main (root-commit) 1e345c7] feat: Add homepage heading\r\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\r\n create mode 100644 index.html\r\nTo github.com:maryam-codes/heritage-portal.git\r\n * [new branch]      main -> main\r\nbranch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.\r\n```\r\n\r\nYour hashes and paths will be different.\r\n\r\n**Maryam invites Bilal:** on GitHub open **Settings → Collaborators → Add people** and type Bilal's GitHub username. Bilal accepts the invitation from his email or GitHub notifications.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Phase 2: Bilal clones and makes a branch\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit clone git@github.com:maryam-codes/heritage-portal.git\r\ncd heritage-portal\r\ngit switch -c feature/landmarks-guide\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput (GitHub also prints a few download progress lines):\r\n\r\n```text\r\nCloning into 'heritage-portal'...\r\nSwitched to a new branch 'feature/landmarks-guide'\r\n```\r\n\r\nBilal never codes straight on `main`. He adds this section to the end of `index.html` in his editor:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<section id=\"landmarks\">\r\n  <h2>Heritage Landmarks</h2>\r\n  <ul>\r\n    <li>Shalimar Bagh (Mughal garden)</li>\r\n    <li>Hari Parbat Fort</li>\r\n    <li>Jamia Masjid</li>\r\n  </ul>\r\n</section>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen he commits and pushes his branch:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit commit -am \"feat: Add heritage landmarks section\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/landmarks-guide\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[feature/landmarks-guide 9b53b1a] feat: Add heritage landmarks section\r\n 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)\r\nTo github.com:maryam-codes/heritage-portal.git\r\n * [new branch]      feature/landmarks-guide -> feature/landmarks-guide\r\nbranch 'feature/landmarks-guide' set up to track 'origin/feature/landmarks-guide'.\r\n```\r\n\r\n`-am` works here because `index.html` is already tracked.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Phase 3: Bilal opens a Pull Request\r\n\r\nOn GitHub:\r\n1. Bilal clicks **Compare & pull request**.\r\n2. Title: `feat: Add heritage landmarks section`.\r\n3. Description:\r\n   > *\"As-salamu alaykum! This PR adds a list of heritage landmarks for visitors. I checked it on a laptop and a phone. Ready for review.\"*\r\n4. He picks Maryam as **Reviewer** and clicks **Create pull request**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Phase 4: Maryam reviews and asks for one change\r\n\r\n1. Maryam opens the **Files changed** tab and reads every line.\r\n2. She comments:\r\n   > *\"MashaAllah, a lovely list! Could we also add Pari Mahal?\"*\r\n3. Bilal adds `<li>Pari Mahal</li>` on his computer, then runs `git commit -am \"feat: Add Pari Mahal to landmarks\"` and `git push`.\r\n4. The PR updates by itself with the new commit.\r\n5. Maryam clicks **Review changes → Approve**, then **Merge pull request**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Phase 5: Both developers update their computers\r\n\r\nThe merge happened on GitHub, so both computers are now behind. Each developer runs:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch main\r\ngit pull\r\ngit branch -d feature/landmarks-guide\r\n```\r\n\r\nBilal's output:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSwitched to branch 'main'\r\nYour branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.\r\nUpdating 1e345c7..2ef6af8\r\nFast-forward\r\n index.html | 9 +++++++++\r\n 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)\r\nDeleted branch feature/landmarks-guide (was d70af73).\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice the second line: Git says \"up to date\" **before** the pull. It only knows what it fetched last time. That is why you always `git pull` before starting new work (Chapter 15).\r\nMaryam never had the feature branch, so she skips `git branch -d`. (The real output also has a line starting `From github.com:...` that shows what was downloaded.)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Phase 6 (optional): Make and solve a merge conflict\r\n\r\n1. **Maryam** changes the heading on `main` to `<h1>Heritage Portal of Srinagar</h1>`, commits and pushes.\r\n2. **Bilal** makes a branch and changes the **same line** to `<h1>Welcome to the Heritage Portal</h1>`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit switch -c feature/welcome-heading\r\ngit commit -am \"feat: Add friendly welcome heading\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Bilal brings Maryam's latest `main` into his branch:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit fetch origin\r\ngit merge origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAuto-merging index.html\r\nCONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in index.html\r\nAutomatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.\r\n```\r\n\r\n4. The top of `index.html` now shows both versions:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n<<<<<<< HEAD\r\n<h1>Welcome to the Heritage Portal</h1>\r\n=======\r\n<h1>Heritage Portal of Srinagar</h1>\r\n>>>>>>> origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\n5. Bilal talks to Maryam, and they agree to keep both ideas. He replaces all five lines with one:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h1>Welcome to the Heritage Portal of Srinagar</h1>\r\n```\r\n\r\n6. He finishes the merge and pushes:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit add index.html\r\ngit commit -m \"merge: Combine both heading ideas\"\r\ngit push -u origin feature/welcome-heading\r\n```\r\n\r\n7. He opens a Pull Request, and Maryam reviews and merges it as in Phases 3 and 4.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🧪 Project Checklist\r\n\r\n- [ ] Nobody pushed straight to `main` while building a feature: every change came through a Pull Request.\r\n- [ ] Bilal's branch was pushed and reviewed in a Pull Request.\r\n- [ ] Every review comment was polite, clear and encouraging (*Adab*).\r\n- [ ] After both developers ran `git pull`, `git log --oneline` on `main` shows the same commits on both computers.\r\n- [ ] `git branch` no longer lists the merged feature branch.\r\n- [ ] (Optional) The conflict was solved, and no `<<<<<<<`, `=======` or `>>>>>>>` lines are left in any file.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A team shares one repository; each person works on their own **feature branch**.\r\n- Changes reach `main` through a **Pull Request** that a teammate reviews kindly.\r\n- Pushing more commits to the same branch updates the open PR.\r\n- After a merge on GitHub, everyone runs `git switch main` and `git pull`, then deletes the old branch with `git branch -d`.\r\n- A conflict happens when two people change the same line. Talk, choose the right lines, remove the markers, then `git add` and `git commit`.\r\n",
  "README.md": "# Git & GitHub for Junior Coders 🐙🚀\r\n\r\nWelcome to the official **Git & GitHub Textbook** of the Junior Coders curriculum!\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Course Overview\r\n\r\nIn modern software development, writing code is only half the adventure. Professional developers must know how to:\r\n1. **Save milestones** and rewind mistakes without losing work.\r\n2. **Branch off** to test wild ideas without crashing the live website.\r\n3. **Collaborate with teammates** across the world on GitHub without overwriting each other's code.\r\n4. **Publish live websites** instantly for the whole world to enjoy using **GitHub Pages**.\r\n\r\nThis book guides you from your very first `git init` to opening professional Pull Requests and building team repositories.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗺️ Curriculum Roadmap\r\n\r\n| Part | Focus | Chapters |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Part 1** | Foundations of Version Control | Ch 1 - Ch 3 |\r\n| **Part 2** | Essential Daily Workflow | Ch 4 - Ch 7 |\r\n| **Part 3** | Time Travel & Safety Nets | Ch 8 - Ch 9 |\r\n| **Part 4** | Branching & Merging | Ch 10 - Ch 12 |\r\n| **Part 5** | GitHub & Remote Repositories | Ch 13 - Ch 16 |\r\n| **Part 6** | Collaboration & Publishing | Ch 17 - Ch 19 |\r\n| **Part 7** | Capstone Team Projects | Projects 1 - 2 |\r\n| **Ref** | Quick Reference & Cheatsheet | Git Command Reference |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Islamic Ethical Framework (*Amānah & Adab*)\r\n\r\nIn accordance with our core guidelines:\r\n- **Protecting Secrets as a Sacred Trust (*Amānah*)**: Never commit `.env` files, passwords, or API keys to GitHub.\r\n- **Dignified Communication (*Adab*)**: Write helpful, respectful commit messages and pull request reviews.\r\n- **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: Use version control to build tools that serve families, schools, and communities.\r\n",
  "reference/git-cheatsheet.md": "# Git & GitHub Quick Reference Cheatsheet ⚡📖\r\n\r\n> **Quick Navigation**: A rapid lookup reference for everyday Git terminal commands, staging, branching, conflict resolution, and GitHub collaboration.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Initial Setup & Identity (One-Time)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Check Git version\r\ngit --version\r\n\r\n# Set global name and email\r\ngit config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\r\ngit config --global user.email \"your.email@srinagar.edu.in\"\r\n\r\n# Set modern default branch name\r\ngit config --global init.defaultBranch main\r\n\r\n# List all configuration settings\r\ngit config --list\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Starting & Inspecting Repositories\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Initialize a new local repository\r\ngit init\r\n\r\n# Clone an existing remote repository\r\ngit clone https://github.com/username/repository.git\r\n\r\n# Check the status of the working tree and staging area\r\ngit status\r\n\r\n# View compact one-line commit history\r\ngit log --oneline\r\n\r\n# View commit history with visual branch graphs\r\ngit log --oneline --graph --all\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. The Daily Commit Loop (Stage & Commit)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Stage a specific file\r\ngit add filename.html\r\n\r\n# Stage all modified and new files\r\ngit add .\r\n\r\n# View unstaged differences\r\ngit diff\r\n\r\n# View staged differences (ready to commit)\r\ngit diff --staged\r\n\r\n# Commit staged files with a meaningful message\r\ngit commit -m \"feat: Add user login form\"\r\n\r\n# Amend the message of the most recent local commit\r\ngit commit --amend -m \"feat: Add responsive user login form\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Undoing Changes & Safety Nets\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Discard unstaged modifications in a file (Back to last commit)\r\ngit restore filename.html\r\n\r\n# Unstage a file (Move out of staging area back to working tree)\r\ngit restore --staged filename.html\r\n\r\n# Temporarily shelve unfinished work\r\ngit stash\r\n\r\n# Restore shelved work\r\ngit stash pop\r\n\r\n# Safely undo a published commit by generating a reverse commit\r\ngit revert <commit-hash>\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Branching & Merging\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# List all local branches\r\ngit branch\r\n\r\n# Create and switch to a new branch\r\ngit switch -c feature/new-navbar\r\n# (Or classic syntax: git checkout -b feature/new-navbar)\r\n\r\n# Switch back to an existing branch\r\ngit switch main\r\n\r\n# Merge a completed feature into current branch\r\ngit merge feature/new-navbar\r\n\r\n# Delete a merged branch\r\ngit branch -d feature/new-navbar\r\n\r\n# Abort a conflicting merge and return to clean state\r\ngit merge --abort\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Remote Synchronization & GitHub\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Link local repository to GitHub\r\ngit remote add origin git@github.com:username/repository.git\r\n\r\n# Verify remote URLs\r\ngit remote -v\r\n\r\n# Push commits and establish upstream tracking\r\ngit push -u origin main\r\n\r\n# Standard push (once upstream is set)\r\ngit push\r\n\r\n# Download remote commits without merging\r\ngit fetch origin\r\n\r\n# Download and merge remote updates into active branch\r\ngit pull origin main\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Conventional Commit Prefix Guide\r\n\r\n| Prefix | When to Use | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `feat:` | A brand-new feature or functionality | `feat: Add student search bar` |\r\n| `fix:` | A bug correction | `fix: Resolve mobile layout overflow` |\r\n| `docs:` | Documentation changes only | `docs: Add deployment instructions to README` |\r\n| `style:`| Code styling, whitespace, CSS formatting | `style: Improve button contrast for accessibility` |\r\n| `refactor:` | Code restructuring with zero behavior change | `refactor: Optimize SQL query joins` |\r\n| `test:` | Adding or fixing test suites | `test: Add validation tests for email format` |\r\n| `chore:` | Build scripts, dependencies, `.gitignore` updates | `chore: Add .DS_Store to .gitignore` |\r\n"
};
